t["zone:ruinsoflordaeron"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="18,495,27.507.0,22.431.0,25.419.1,48.191.0,61.370.0,23.229.0,49.228.0,61.375.1,54.232.0",["h"]="d32dbe",["n"]="Ruinas de Lordaeron",["s"]="Ruinas de Lordaeron. Encaramadas directamente sobre la subterránea Entrañas, las Ruinas de Lordaeron son los restos desolados del que fuera el poderoso reino septentrional de la humanidad. Caídas ante la Plaga y el príncipe Arthas durante la Tercera Guerra, por las calles de la superficie y las plazas resquebrajadas merodean ahora monstruosidades errantes, espectros vengativos y viles experimentadores. Tanto la Alianza como la Horda envían expediciones a esta ciudadela asolada para recuperar reliquias del pasado caído o purgar los horrores que acechan sobre la capital de los Renegados. La capital de Lordaeron fue en su momento la joya de la corona de los Reinos del Este, la sede del rey Terenas Menethil II y el corazón palpitante de la civilización humana. Cuando el príncipe Arthas regresó de Rasganorte, asesinó a su padre en la sala del trono y desató a los no-muertos sobre los ciudadanos. Hoy, mientras los Renegados habitan en las catacumbas de Entrañas abajo, las ruinas de la superficie siguen siendo una zona en penumbra abandonada a su suerte. Los arcos derruidos y los adoquines cubiertos de maleza cobijan a bestias ferales como Witherfang y a trastornados vestigios del pasado.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,1,0},["sec"]={0,0,1}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Paladin"]="Lordaeron was the cradle of the Knights of the Silver Hand; walking its desecrated halls to purge horrors and lay restless captains to rest is a solemn duty of the Light.",["faction:Alliance"]="Venturing into Lordaeron allows the Alliance to strike at the threshold of the Forsaken capital while bringing justice and peace to the spirits of their slaughtered northern brothers.",["race:Human"]="Human adventurers view the ruined capital as hallowed ground stolen by betrayal, seeking to reclaim lost heirlooms and honor fallen kin.",["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, the ruins above are their former home and eternal graveyard. Clearing it secures their front door and supplies the apothecaries with components to develop the New Plague."},["faq"]={{["a"]="The dungeon is situated directly on the surface levels of Capital City in Tirisfal Glades, sitting right above the entrance network that leads down into the Forsaken capital of the Undercity.",["al"]={"ruins of lordaeron location","where is lordaeron dungeon","undercity dungeon entrance"},["q"]="Where is the Ruins of Lordaeron dungeon located?"},{["a"]="For the Alliance, venturing into Lordaeron is a sacred pilgrimage to recover lost family heirlooms, collect royal crests, and grant final peace to fallen ancestors. For the Horde, particularly the Forsaken, clearing out the upper ruins is essential for securing the perimeter of the Undercity and acquiring research samples for the Royal Apothecary Society's new plague.",["al"]={"why go to lordaeron ruins","alliance quests lordaeron","horde quests lordaeron"},["q"]="Why are both the Horde and Alliance interested in this place?"},{["a"]="Viktor the Vile is one of the malevolent figures taking advantage of the ruins' lawlessness, operating foul experiments and contributing to the festering corruption that keeps the upper capital infested with twisted abominations.",["al"]={"viktor the vile lore","who is viktor the vile"},["q"]="Who is Viktor the Vile?"},{["a"]="The Abandoned is a grotesque undead monstrosity left behind in the ruins. Like many patchwork horrors created during and after the Scourge's conquest, it wanders the derelict chambers aimlessly, attacking anything it encounters.",["al"]={"the abandoned boss","who is the abandoned"},["q"]="What is 'The Abandoned' encountered in the ruins?"},{["a"]="Defeating the spectral Lordaeron Captain releases a high-ranking royal defender from a perpetual, maddening duty. Bound by honor since the Scourge's initial massacre, the Captain has spent years guarding a destroyed kingdom against all who approach. Laying the Captain to rest breaks an agonizing cycle of phantom servitude and allows their bloodied insignia to be reclaimed.",["al"]={"defeating lordaeron captain","lordaeron captain significance","what happens when captain dies"},["q"]="What does defeating the Lordaeron Captain mean for the story?"},{["a"]="Yes. While the Forsaken make their home in the Undercity caverns below, the surface ruins are an active combat zone overrun with restless ghosts, feral predators, and experimental threats that the Forsaken have not fully eradicated.",["al"]={"is undercity in this dungeon","ruins vs undercity"},["q"]="Is the Ruins of Lordaeron separate from the Undercity?"}},["kw"]={"bjork","lordaeron captain","new plague","rath'mael","ruins of lordaeron","the abandoned","the baron","tirisfal glades","undercity","viktor the vile","witherfang"},["n"]="Ruins of Lordaeron",["s"]="Perched directly above the subterranean Undercity, the Ruins of Lordaeron are the hollow remains of humanity's once-mighty northern kingdom. Fallen to the Scourge and Prince Arthas during the Third War, the surface streets and cracked plazas now crawl with wandering monstrosities, vengeful specters, and vile experimenters. Both the Alliance and the Horde dispatch ventures into this blighted citadel to reclaim relics of the fallen past or purge the horrors lurking above the Forsaken capital.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Capital City was once the crown jewel of the Eastern Kingdoms, the seat of King Terenas Menethil II and the beating heart of human civilization. When Prince Arthas returned from Northrend, he slew his father in the throne room and unleashed the undead upon the citizenry. Today, while the Forsaken dwell in the catacombs of the Undercity below, the surface ruins remain a forsaken twilight zone. Broken archways and weed-choked cobbles shelter feral beasts like Witherfang and deranged remnants of the past.",["t"]="A Fallen Empire's Courtyard"},{["b"]="The dangers within the ruined grounds go beyond restless dead. Apothecaries and sinister figures like Viktor the Vile work on testing new plagues among the shadows, while twisted experiments like The Abandoned roam freely. Wrathful spirits such as Rath'mael and cursed retainers like The Baron haunt the collapsed hallways, locked in unending torment and striking out against any living or undead trespassers who cross their paths.",["t"]="Torment and Pestilence"},{["b"]="Among the most poignant hazards in the upper ruins are the spectral remnants of the city's defenders, culminating in the appearance of a ghostly Lordaeron Captain. Still clinging to their shattered oaths, these apparitions perceive anyone approaching the throne as invaders. Putting the Captain and their soldiers to rest brings a bittersweet closure to an unbroken vigil that has lasted since the day the capital fell.",["t"]="Ghosts of the Royal Guard"}}}}}
t["zone:scarlet"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="17,466,18.553.0,14.615.0,31.585.1,66.315.0,55.420.1,46.238.0,46.273.0,57.303.1,74.330.0",["h"]="2d6dbe",["n"]="Monasterio Escarlata",["s"]="Monasterio Escarlata. Encaramado en las colinas del noreste de Claros de Tirisfal, el Monasterio Escarlata fue en su día un apacible santuario y centro de conocimiento para la Iglesia de la Luz Sagrada. Tras la caída de Lordaeron, la Cruzada Escarlata se apoderó de él y lo fortificó con celo para convertirlo en su principal base avanzada en el oeste. En la actualidad se erige como una extensa fortaleza dividida en cuatro alas, donde fanáticos devotos entrenan, torturan a cautivos y libran una guerra despiadada contra todo forastero. Antes de la Tercera Guerra, el monasterio sirvió como orgullosa sede del clero de Lordaeron y seminario para los incipientes paladines de la Mano de Plata, un lugar muy querido en su momento por el mismísimo arzobispo Alonsus Faol. Tras la devastación de la Plaga, los cruzados supervivientes purgaron las estancias de los no-muertos y arañas rezagados, transformando los tranquilos archivos y patios en una guarnición fortificada. Consumida por la paranoia y el dolor, la Cruzada Escarlata pasó a considerar a todo extraño —vivo o no-muerto, humano o de cualquier otra raza— como un portador de la peste al que se debía purificar con fuego.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,1,0,0,1,0},["sec"]={0,0,1}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Mage"]="The Library wing houses rare historical and theological tomes guarded by Arcanist Doan, a former Dalaran scholar who defected to the Crusade's cause.",["class:Paladin"]="The monastery once served as a sacred seminary where the first paladins of the Silver Hand trained and prayed, making its descent into crimson zealotry a tragic reminder of how devotion can curdle into madness.",["class:Priest"]="Originally dedicated to Archbishop Alonsus Faol and the Church of the Holy Light, its grand chapels demonstrate how holy power can still be wielded by mortals twisted by hatred and vengeance.",["race:Undead"]="Perched on the border of Tirisfal Glades, the Scarlet Monastery represents an existential threat to the Forsaken, staging spies into Deathknell and torturing captured undead within its dungeons."},["faq"]={{["a"]="The Crusade has succumbed to extreme madness and paranoia. In their eyes, anyone who does not wear their crimson tabard is a potential carrier of the Scourge plague. They no longer make distinctions between friend or foe, viewing all outsiders as tainted beings who must be purged before they can spread corruption.",["al"]={"why do scarlets attack humans","scarlet crusade hostile alliance","why are they mad"},["q"]="Why is the Scarlet Crusade hostile to living Alliance adventurers?"},{["a"]="Defeating High Inquisitor Whitemane decapitates the Scarlet Crusade's western military leadership. As the spiritual leader of the monastery, her miraculous resurrection powers maintained troop morale and kept Commander Mograine fighting at her side. Her death removes the Crusade's most potent priestess in Tirisfal Glades, leaving the garrison disordered and severely diminishing their threat to the surrounding region.",["al"]={"what does killing whitemane mean","whitemane defeated lore","cathedral ending"},["q"]="What happens when you defeat High Inquisitor Whitemane?"},{["a"]="The Scarlet Key is held deep within the Library wing of the monastery, kept inside Arcanist Doan's strongbox behind his personal study. Possessing this key is necessary to unlock the outer gates of both the Armory and the Cathedral wings.",["al"]={"where is scarlet key","how to unlock armory and cathedral","monastery key"},["q"]="What is the Scarlet Key and where is it found?"},{["a"]="Arcanist Doan was a gifted human wizard from Dalaran who abandoned his fellow Kirin Tor magi to align with the Scarlet Crusade. Seeking seclusion to study rare manuscripts and master powerful fire and arcane magic, he took control of the monastery's extensive Library wing.",["al"]={"who is arcanist doan","doan lore","mage in library"},["q"]="Why is Arcanist Doan in the Scarlet Monastery?"},{["a"]="Alexandros Mograine, the famed Ashbringer and former leader of the Silver Hand in Lordaeron, prayed and trained at the monastery during his youth. After the Third War, he made it a forward staging ground against the undead; his son Renault now commands the Cathedral alongside High Inquisitor Whitemane.",["al"]={"mograine monastery history","ashbringer monastery","renault mograine father"},["q"]="Who was Alexandros Mograine and how is he connected here?"},{["a"]="While the rest of the monastery maintains an outward appearance of pristine devotion, the Graveyard is where the Crusade's fanaticism rots from within. It holds their brutal torture chambers run by Interrogator Vishas, while neglected crypts and restless dead are harvested or manipulated by fallen casters like Bloodmage Thalnos.",["al"]={"why is graveyard undead","thalnos lore","interrogator vishas"},["q"]="What makes the Graveyard wing so different from the rest of the monastery?"}},["kw"]={"armory","cathedral","doan","graveyard","herod","library","mograine","scarlet crusade","scarlet monastery","tirisfal glades","whitemane"},["n"]="Scarlet Monastery",["s"]="Perched in the northeastern hills of Tirisfal Glades, the Scarlet Monastery was once a peaceful sanctuary and center of learning for the Church of the Holy Light. Following the fall of Lordaeron, it was seized and heavily fortified by the Scarlet Crusade as their primary forward base in the west. Now, it stands as a sprawling stronghold divided into four wings, where fanatical zealots train, torture captives, and wage an uncompromising war against all outsiders.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Before the Third War, the monastery served as a proud seat of Lordaeron's priesthood and a seminary for fledgling paladins of the Silver Hand, once cherished by Archbishop Alonsus Faol himself. Following the Scourge's devastation, surviving crusaders purged the halls of lingering undead and spiders, transforming the tranquil archives and courtyards into a fortified garrison. Driven by paranoia and grief, the Scarlet Crusade came to view all outsiders—living or undead, human or otherwise—as carriers of the plague who must be cleansed with fire.",["t"]="A Fallen Sanctuary"},{["b"]="Past the monastery's Grand Vestibule lie four distinct quarters. The Graveyard is an unhallowed open-air terrace where fallen crusaders rise as undead abominations and torturers pry confessions from prisoners. The Library preserves precious theological texts and historical tomes, guarded by the renegade mage Arcanist Doan and his hounds. The Armory serves as a training ground where Scarlet weaponmasters like Herod drill fanatic recruits into ironclad warriors. Deepest within stands the Cathedral, the grand heart of the stronghold where the Crusade's leadership directs their crusade across the blighted north.",["t"]="The Four Wings"},{["b"]="The inner sanctum of the Cathedral is commanded by Scarlet Commander Mograine and the monastery's true master, High Inquisitor Sally Whitemane. Defeating Whitemane strikes a critical blow to the Crusade's operational command in Tirisfal Glades. Her ability to channel blinding Holy Light and resurrect fallen champions made the garrison seem invincible to its devotees. Slaying her shatters the aura of divine mandate that fueled the monastery's zealotry, exposing the Crusade's leadership as mortal and severely weakening their campaign against the Forsaken and the neighboring lands.",["t"]="The Fall of the High Inquisitor"}}}}}
t["zone:searinggorge"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="13,419,20.591.0,29.570.0,39.591.0,51.408.0,44.334.0,33.304.0,50.349.0,45.248.0,62.233.0",["h"]="6846a6",["n"]="La Garganta de Fuego",["s"]="La Garganta de Fuego. La Garganta de Fuego es un cañón calcinado y desolado situado en el sur de Khaz Modan, que limita al sur con la Montaña Roca Negra. En su día fue una verde extensión de las Montañas Crestagrana, pero quedó devastada hace más de dos siglos por la apocalíptica invocación de Ragnaros, el Señor del Fuego. En la actualidad, la garganta está intensamente explotada por el clan Hierro Negro, cuyas extensas minas con esclavos en La Caldera están estrechamente vigiladas por la escindida Hermandad del torio. Antes de la Guerra de los Tres Martillos, el territorio que hoy conforma La Garganta de Fuego era una región montañosa, verde y ondulada, unida a la cordillera de Crestagrana. Ese exuberante paisaje dejó de existir hace más de doscientos treinta años, cuando el señor feudal hechicero Thaurissan, desesperado por cambiar el rumbo de la derrota, invocó a Ragnaros desde las profundidades del mundo. El cataclísmico surgimiento del Señor del Fuego desgarró la tierra, alzó la Montaña Roca Negra, redujo la gran ciudad de Thaurissan de los Hierro Negro a ruinas humeantes y convirtió el valle septentrional en una extensión resquebrajada de ceniza, azufre y vidrio volcánico.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,0}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Rogue"]="Rogues with seasoned lockpicking skill are valued travelers here, as they can bypass the locked Stonewrought Pass gate connecting Loch Modan directly to the gorge.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance keeps the gate at Stonewrought Pass locked to isolate the Dark Irons from peaceful dwarven settlements in Loch Modan, though courageous adventurers are sanctioned to strike into the gorge.",["faction:Horde"]="Using Kargath as a staging base, the Horde cut an overland approach into the gorge to gather intelligence on Blackrock Mountain and disrupt Dark Iron operations in Khaz Modan.",["profession:Mining"]="The volcanic landscape and deep excavations in the Slag Pit make Searing Gorge one of the primary hunting grounds for rich veins of mithril, truesilver, and coveted Dark Iron ore.",["race:Dwarf"]="For dwarves of Ironforge, Searing Gorge is a bitter reminder of the War of the Three Hammers and the catastrophic folly of the Dark Iron clan, whose excavations now threaten Khaz Modan's southern approaches."},["faq"]={{["a"]="Over two hundred and thirty years ago, during the War of the Three Hammers, the Dark Iron Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan attempted to summon a supernatural power to avoid defeat. Instead, he summoned Ragnaros the Firelord. The cataclysmic eruption shattered the southern Redridge range, created the volcano of Blackrock Mountain, and incinerated the surrounding valleys into Searing Gorge and the Burning Steppes.",["al"]={"why is it burnt","searing gorge lore","how was searing gorge created"},["q"]="Why is the Searing Gorge burned and covered in ash?"},{["a"]="They belong to the Thorium Brotherhood, an independent faction of Dark Iron dwarves who severed ties with their imperial kin under Blackrock Mountain. From their camp atop Thorium Point, they work as master smiths and artificers, trading knowledge and crafting secrets to any traveler who aids them against the Dark Iron forces below.",["al"]={"thorium point dwarves","who is thorium brotherhood","thorium brotherhood lore"},["q"]="Who are the dwarves living at Thorium Point?"},{["a"]="The Cauldron is a massive open excavation dominated by the Dark Iron clan, containing the subterranean Slag Pit. Dark Iron overseers and war golems use captured prisoners as slave labor to extract precious ores—including valuable Dark Iron deposits—to feed the great forges inside Blackrock Mountain.",["al"]={"what is the cauldron","the slag pit lore","what are dark irons mining"},["q"]="What is going on in the Cauldron?"},{["a"]="The passage from Loch Modan through Stonewrought Pass is barred by a heavy locked gate guarded by Ironforge mountaineers. Travelers must either obtain the Key to Searing Gorge through an official task for Curator Thorius, have a skilled rogue pick the mechanism, or bypass the gate entirely by taking the open trail south of Kargath in the Badlands.",["al"]={"stonewrought pass gate","how to open searing gorge door","key to searing gorge"},["q"]="How do you get into Searing Gorge from Loch Modan?"},{["a"]="Operating from the nearby fortress of Kargath in the Badlands, Horde commanders carved a mountain pass into the gorge to scout Dark Iron troop movements and secure tactical routes toward Blackrock Mountain, which holds immense strategic importance.",["al"]={"why is horde here","kargath gorge connection","horde presence"},["q"]="Why is the Horde interested in Searing Gorge?"},{["a"]="There are no instance portals directly in the gorge itself, but the southern entrance leads straight into Blackrock Mountain. That mountain houses Blackrock Depths, Blackrock Spire, and the deep passages leading into the Molten Core.",["al"]={"searing gorge instances","dungeons in searing gorge","where is molten core"},["q"]="Are there any dungeons or raid entrances inside Searing Gorge?"}},["kw"]={"blackrock mountain","cauldron","dark iron clan","ragnaros","searing gorge","slag pit","stonewrought pass","thorium brotherhood","thorium point"},["n"]="Searing Gorge",["s"]="Searing Gorge is a scorched, desolate canyon in southern Khaz Modan, bordering Blackrock Mountain to the south. Once a verdant expanse of the Redridge Mountains, it was devastated over two centuries ago by the apocalyptic summoning of Ragnaros the Firelord. Today, the gorge is heavily worked by the Dark Iron clan, whose extensive slave mines in the Cauldron are closely monitored by the breakaway Thorium Brotherhood.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Before the War of the Three Hammers, the territory that is now Searing Gorge was a green, rolling mountain region connected to the Redridge chain. That lush landscape ceased to exist over two hundred and thirty years ago when Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan, desperate to turn the tide of defeat, summoned Ragnaros from the depths beneath the world. The cataclysmic emergence of the Firelord tore the earth asunder, raised Blackrock Mountain, reduced the grand Dark Iron city of Thaurissan to smoking ruins, and burned the northern valley into a cracked expanse of ash, sulfur, and volcanic glass.",["t"]="A Scorched Wasteland"},{["b"]="Seeking raw materials to supply their war machine after the loss of their old capital, the Dark Iron dwarves seized the desolate gorge. In the center of the valley lies the Cauldron, a colossal open-pit excavation plunging deep into the stone. Within its lower recesses sits the Slag Pit, a brutal subterranean mining complex. Here, Dark Iron overseers and lumbering war golems drive captured enemies into grueling slave labor, pulling iron, mithril, and rare deposits of Dark Iron ore out of the burning rock under the constant threat of lava surges and roaming beasts.",["t"]="The Cauldron and the Slag Pit"},{["b"]="Perched on the northern crags overlooking the excavation sits Thorium Point, the fortified outpost of the Thorium Brotherhood. Having split from their kin in Blackrock Mountain, these master craftsmen offer neutral shelter to travelers willing to undermine the Dark Iron operations or supply them with rare smelting components. Access to the gorge itself is tightly restricted: the Alliance keeps the Stonewrought Pass locked down behind heavy iron gates toward Loch Modan, while Horde expeditions from nearby Kargath have cut a narrow trail through the eastern badlands to scout the region.",["t"]="Thorium Brotherhood and Contested Paths"}}}}}
t["zone:shendralas"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="11,286,23.441.0,36.393.0,19.282.0,29.189.0,55.155.0,45.172.0,27.175.0,28.139.0,57.153.0",["h"]="18acf1",["n"]="Shen'dralas",["s"]="Shen'dralas. Shen'dralas es una tierra salvaje, escarpada y disputada situada entre Feralas, Mulgore y el sureste de Desolace. Dominada por antiguos vestigios de los elfos de la noche y fronteras conflictivas, sirve como una encrucijada inestable donde chocan centauros invasores, desterrados desplazados y erizados jabaespines. Escondida en las alturas al sureste de Desolace y bordeada por Mulgore y Feralas, Shen'dralas es una tierra fronteriza agreste, muy alejada de las capitales de las principales facciones. Sus desfiladeros escarpados y enclaves ocultos albergan a grupos desesperados que se labran la vida al borde del Kalimdor salvaje. Desde miradores apartados como Evenshade Overlook hasta refugios sin ley como el Escondrijo de los Desterrados, la zona se define por aquellos que desean vigilar sin ser vistos o permanecer en el olvido.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,0}},["text"]={["ang"]={["faction:Alliance"]="A remote, contested wilderness providing alternate routes between Feralas and central Kalimdor, fraught with dangers that require keen vigilance.",["faction:Horde"]="Positioned just outside the southern reaches of Mulgore, keeping the Magram and quilboar contained here is vital for protecting Horde frontiers.",["race:NightElf"]="The name evokes the ancient Highborne lore of Eldre'Thalas, drawing the attention of sentinels wary of reckless arcana in southern Kalimdor.",["race:Tauren"]="With the region bordering Mulgore and Desolace, the aggression of quilboar and centaur along these ridges poses a direct threat to nearby ancestral hunting grounds."},["faq"]={{["a"]="Shen'dralas lies south-east of Desolace, nestled between the borders of Feralas and Mulgore. Travelers arriving on foot typically enter from Desolace by passing through the Valley of Bones.",["al"]={"where is shendralas","shendralas location","how to get to shendralas"},["q"]="Where is Shen'dralas located?"},{["a"]="Neither faction holds dominion here. Shen'dralas is a contested frontier populated by roving centaur, hostile quilboar, outcasts, and isolated encampments.",["al"]={"is shendralas horde","is shendralas alliance","who owns shendralas"},["q"]="Is Shen'dralas controlled by the Alliance or the Horde?"},{["a"]="Prominent landmarks include the Valley of Bones, Bristleback Retreat, Evenshade Overlook, the Magram Front, Outcast Hideaway, and the central expanse of Shen'dralas itself.",["al"]={"shendralas subzones","landmarks in shendralas","points of interest shendralas"},["q"]="What major settlements or landmarks are here?"},{["a"]="Centaur territorial wars extend from the plains of Desolace straight into the region along the Magram Front, where the clan clashes against local defenders and rival factions.",["al"]={"centaur in shendralas","magram front","why are magram here"},["q"]="Why are there centaur here?"},{["a"]="Bristleback Retreat is a fortified outpost held by Bristleback quilboar who have established a fierce foothold along the edges of the zone.",["al"]={"bristleback retreat","quilboar in shendralas"},["q"]="What is Bristleback Retreat?"},{["a"]="Geographically, the wilderness of Shen'dralas sits across the mountainous territory where the secluded Lake Dumont sits tucked away in old survey charts.",["al"]={"lake dumont","shendralas map history"},["q"]="What is the connection between this zone and Lake Dumont?"}},["kw"]={"bristleback retreat","desolace","evenshade overlook","feralas","lake dumont","magram front","mulgore","outcast hideaway","shendralas","valley of bones"},["n"]="Shen'dralas",["s"]="Shen'dralas is a rugged, contested wilderness situated between Feralas, Mulgore, and south-eastern Desolace. Dominated by ancient night elven remnants and troubled borders, it serves as a volatile crossroads where encroaching centaur, displaced outcasts, and bristling quilboar collide.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Tucked away in the heights south-east of Desolace and bordered by Mulgore and Feralas, Shen'dralas is a raw borderland far removed from major faction capitols. Its craggy passes and hidden enclaves harbor desperate groups carving out existence on the edge of wild Kalimdor. From secluded vantage points like Evenshade Overlook to lawless refuges like Outcast Hideaway, the zone is defined by those who wish to watch unseen or stay forgotten.",["t"]="A Borderland in Turmoil"},{["b"]="The land bears the distinct mark of the highborne legacy that gave it its name, yet it is far from peaceful. Centaur conflict bleeds across the Magram Front, keeping old blood feuds alive and well along its borders. Meanwhile, encroaching quilboar maintain fortified redoubts such as Bristleback Retreat, turning every shadowed ravine into contested territory for any traveler passing through.",["t"]="Centaur, Quilboar, and Ancient Ruins"},{["b"]="Leading out north-west toward Desolace lies the Valley of Bones, a grim corridor that serves as the primary overland access route into Shen'dralas. Bleached by sun and wind, this passage stands as a harsh reminder of the violent struggles that constantly ripple through these crags.",["t"]="The Valley of Bones"}}}}}
t["zone:silverpine"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="17,362,18.611.0,25.536.0,30.560.1,49.350.0,57.329.0,23.292.0,35.268.0,22.284.0,48.260.0",["h"]="52e0ef",["n"]="Bosque de Argénteos",["s"]="Bosque de Argénteos. El Bosque de Argénteos es un bosque inquietante y envuelto en niebla que se extiende a lo largo de la costa occidental de Lordaeron, entre Claros de Tirisfal y la cerrada Muralla de Cringrís. Asolado por las cicatrices persistentes de la peste propagada por la Plaga, sirve como la disputada frontera sur de los Renegados. Quienes viajan por aquí se enfrentan a feroces huargen que merodean entre las sombras, gnolls enfermos y enclaves humanos hostiles. Antaño celebrado como una frondosa maravilla de Lordaeron por la que los nobles viajaban hacia Ciudad Capital, el Bosque de Argénteos se ha marchitado bajo el impacto de la Tercera Guerra. Nieblas espectrales serpentean entre pinos agonizantes de corteza plateada, y granjas en ruinas yacen abandonadas a merced de la naturaleza. Al norte se encuentran los Claros de Tirisfal, mientras que la orilla este limita con el Lago Lordamere. Al sur se alza la imponente y sellada Muralla de Cringrís, que aísla al reino aislacionista de Gilneas del caos circundante. Los Renegados mantienen su puesto principal en El Sepulcro, esforzándose por asegurar el bosque frente a enemigos por todos los frentes.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,1}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Mage"]="The presence of Ambermill and the fall of Archmage Arugal into lupine madness make Silverpine a grim cautionary tale of Kirin Tor magic gone disastrously astray.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance explorers will find a profoundly hostile forest, punctuated only by a fragile Dalaran garrison at Ambermill and the tragic, cursed villagers of Pyrewood Village.",["race:Undead"]="Silverpine represents the immediate front line of Forsaken expansion south of Tirisfal Glades, where the Royal Apothecary Society tests new concoctions and the Deathguard battles lingering Scourge agents."},["faq"]={{["a"]="The Greymane Wall is a massive stone barrier built by King Genn Greymane to completely isolate his kingdom of Gilneas from the rest of Lordaeron. When the Scourge ravaged the continent during the Third War, Gilneas locked its gates and refused outside aid, leaving the lands north of the wall—including southern Silverpine—to fend for themselves.",["al"]={"why is there a giant wall","greymane wall lore","gate to gilneas"},["q"]="What is the Greymane Wall at the southern border?"},{["a"]="The inhabitants of Pyrewood Village suffer from Archmage Arugal's worgen curse. By day, they retain human form and maintain their daily routines, remaining friendly to the Alliance. When the moon rises, the curse takes hold, transforming the entire population into savage, bloodthirsty worgen that violently attack anyone nearby.",["al"]={"pyrewood village mystery","pyrewood worgen","why are pyrewood humans hostile at night"},["q"]="Why do the villagers of Pyrewood Village attack at night?"},{["a"]="Thule Ravenclaw is a powerful necromancer serving the Scourge. Operating out of Fenris Keep on Fenris Isle within Lordamere Lake, he commands legions of undead and exerts influence over the diseased Rot Hide gnolls across eastern Silverpine, directly threatening the Forsaken's eastern flank.",["al"]={"who leads fenris isle","thule ravenclaw lore","scourge on fenris isle"},["q"]="Who is Thule Ravenclaw?"},{["a"]="Ambermill is held by wizards of Dalaran led by Archmage Ataeric. Operating from the village, these human mages monitor Forsaken movements, study the magical afflictions plaguing the forest, and seek to contain the nearby worgen outbreak radiating from Shadowfang Keep.",["al"]={"ambermill mages","why is the alliance in ambermill","dalaran in silverpine"},["q"]="Why is Dalaran occupying Ambermill?"},{["a"]="The Sepulcher is the primary Forsaken military outpost and travel hub in Silverpine Forest. Under the command of High Executor Hadrec, it serves as the base of operations for Deathguard patrols, Deathstalker scouts, and the Royal Apothecary Society as they fight to secure the forest.",["al"]={"horde town in silverpine","where is the flight path","sepulcher lore"},["q"]="What is the Sepulcher?"},{["a"]="Silverpine Forest has no Alliance quest hubs or friendly towns; all local quests are exclusively for the Horde. Alliance adventurers typically only enter the forest when traveling from Southshore to explore Shadowfang Keep or to battle hostile Forsaken forces.",["al"]={"is silverpine horde only","alliance quests in silverpine","can alliance go to silverpine"},["q"]="Can Alliance players quest in Silverpine Forest?"}},["kw"]={"ambermill","arugal","fenris isle","greymane wall","pyrewood","rot hide","sepulcher","shadowfang","silverpine","silverpine forest"},["n"]="Silverpine Forest",["s"]="Silverpine Forest is an eerie, mist-laden woodland stretching along Lordaeron's western coast between Tirisfal Glades and the sealed Greymane Wall. Haunted by the lingering scars of the Scourge plague, it serves as the contested southern frontier of the Forsaken. Travelers here face feral worgen roaming the shadows, diseased gnolls, and hostile human enclaves.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Once celebrated as a lush wonder of Lordaeron through which noble travelers journeyed to Capital City, Silverpine Forest has withered under the touch of the Third War. Ghostly fogs weave between dying, silver-barked pines, and ruined farmsteads sit abandoned to the wilderness. To the north lies Tirisfal Glades, while the eastern shore borders Lordamere Lake. To the south stands the imposing and shut Greymane Wall, sealing off the isolationist kingdom of Gilneas from the surrounding chaos. The Forsaken maintain their central outpost at the Sepulcher, striving to secure the woods from enemies on all sides.",["t"]="A Haunted Frontier"},{["b"]="Silverpine's most infamous peril is the worgen curse. High above the southwest woods sits Shadowfang Keep, the former seat of Baron Silverlaine, now dominated by the mad Dalaran archmage Arugal. Seeking to repel the Scourge during the Third War, Arugal summoned extra-dimensional wolf-beasts. The creatures turned on their summoners, spreading a lycanthropic curse across the southern woods. In Pyrewood Village, ordinary-looking human villagers go about their chores by daylight, but transform into savage worgen beneath the night sky.",["t"]="The Worgen and Shadowfang"},{["b"]="The Forsaken claim to Silverpine is hotly contested by living and undead factions alike. To the east on Fenris Isle, Thule Ravenclaw leads a force of Scourge remnants while commanding the plague-addled Rot Hide gnolls that plague northern farmlands. In the south, human mages from Dalaran hold the fortified settlement of Ambermill, conducting mystical research and opposing Forsaken expansion. The Royal Apothecary Society and High Executor Hadrec at the Sepulcher wage a brutal, multifaceted campaign to eliminate these threats and test virulent new plagues.",["t"]="Rival Enclaves and the Scourge"}}}}}
t["zone:stockade"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="12,455,20.556.0,20.705.1,30.341.1,38.314.0,35.198.0,60.303.0,60.167.0,61.278.0,37.226.0",["h"]="d23396",["n"]="Las Mazmorras",["s"]="Las Mazmorras. Las Mazmorras son el complejo penitenciario de alta seguridad de Ventormenta, escondido bajo el distrito de los canales de la ciudad, cerca del Barrio de los Magos. Construidas para albergar a los asesinos más peligrosos del reino, insurgentes políticos y enemigos del estado capturados, las instalaciones han caído recientemente en una violenta anarquía. El alcaide Thelagua se ha visto obligado a retroceder hasta la entrada, reclutando desesperadamente a aventureros capaces para sofocar el motín desde dentro antes de que se extienda a la capital. Bajo los tranquilos canales de Ventormenta, Las Mazmorras se han convertido en una zona de guerra. Cuando el ejército regular marchó a luchar en frentes lejanos, la prisión, falta de personal, dependió de jóvenes inexpertos —incluidos reclutas de catorce años— para mantener el orden. No aguantaron. Los reclusos iniciaron una rebelión sangrienta y coordinada, rasgando sus mantas de lana para hacer pañuelos que identificaran a su banda y aniquilando a los guardias restantes. El alcaide Thelagua apenas pudo mantener el control de la entrada, dejando las celdas, los pasillos y los bloques de detención en las despiadadas manos de los convictos.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,1,1}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Mage"]="Located right on the canal edge of the Mage Quarter, the prison contains physical brawlers vulnerable to slowing spells, while the ogre mage Hamhock can unleash Chain Lightning.",["class:Rogue"]="The prison layout presents an intricate web of locked iron cells and Defias connections, offering unique lockpicking practice and insight into Bazil Thredd's underworld syndicate.",["faction:Alliance"]="The rebellion threatens the very heart of the capital; putting down the uprising preserves Stormwind's internal security while honoring off-the-books bounties for Redridge, Duskwood, and Ironforge.",["race:Dwarf"]="The presence of Kam Deepfury in Stormwind's cells is an affront to Dun Modr; the Kingdom of Ironforge demands his head to avenge dwarven lives lost in the Wetlands."},["faq"]={{["a"]="The Defias Brotherhood orchestrated a prison-wide riot while the facility was severely understaffed. With the Stormwind Army deployed to distant battlefronts, teenage volunteers were left guarding hardened killers. The convicts ambushed the staff, seized weapons, and forced Warden Thelwater out of the cellblocks.",["al"]={"why riot","what happened in stockades","stockades riot cause"},["q"]="Why is the Stockade in open rebellion?"},{["a"]="The mastermind behind the revolt is Bazil Thredd, an influential Defias felon incarcerated in the eastern cellblock. Other dangerous prisoners include Bruegal Ironknuckle, Hamhock, and Kam Deepfury.",["al"]={"stockades boss","who leads the riot","who runs stockades"},["q"]="Who is running the uprising inside?"},{["a"]="Both were condemned to death, but corrupt politicians within the House of Nobles placed bureaucratic holds on their executions to use them as political leverage. This interference outraged allies in Lakeshire and Darkshire, prompting them to send adventurers to eliminate the prisoners during the chaos.",["al"]={"why wasn't targorr executed","dextren ward court","house of nobles corruption"},["q"]="Why are prisoners like Targorr and Dextren Ward still alive?"},{["a"]="Bruegal Ironknuckle is a dangerous dwarven brawler among the rioters. Defeating him removes one threat from the prison while adventurers work to put down the uprising.",["al"]={"bruegal ironknuckle lore","kill bruegal","who is bruegal"},["q"]="What does defeating Bruegal Ironknuckle mean for the prison?"},{["a"]="The Stockade serves as a maximum-security holding pen for high-value prisoners of war. Targorr the Dread led Blackrock orc attacks in Redridge, while Kam Deepfury conducted sabotage for the Dark Iron clan in the Wetlands before both were captured and transferred to the capital.",["al"]={"why dark irons here","kam deepfury lore","targorr stockades"},["q"]="Why are there Dark Iron dwarves and orcs inside a human jail?"},{["a"]="Yes, but it is exceptionally hazardous. The dungeon entrance sits directly inside Stormwind City near the Mage Quarter canals. Horde infiltrators must sneak past the city guards and the capital's defenders to reach the portal.",["al"]={"can horde do stockades","horde in stormwind prison"},["q"]="Can Horde players enter the Stockade?"}},["kw"]={"bazil thredd","bruegal ironknuckle","canals","defias","dextren ward","hamhock","kam deepfury","riot","stockade","stormwind","targorr","warden thelwater"},["n"]="The Stockade",["s"]="The Stockade is Stormwind's high-security prison complex, tucked beneath the city's canal district near the Mage Quarter. Built to hold the kingdom's most dangerous cutthroats, political insurgents, and captured enemies of state, the facility has recently collapsed into violent anarchy. Warden Thelwater has been forced back to the threshold, desperately recruiting capable adventurers to crush the riot from within before it spills out into the capital.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Beneath the tranquil canals of Stormwind, the Stockade has become a war zone. When the regular army marched out to fight on distant fronts, the understaffed prison leaned on inexperienced youth—including fourteen-year-old recruits—to maintain order. It did not hold. The inmates launched a bloody, coordinated rebellion, tearing their wool blankets into gang scarves and cutting down the remaining guards. Warden Thelwater barely maintained control of the entryway, leaving the cells, hallways, and holding blocks in the ruthless hands of the convicts.",["t"]="A Prison Under Siege"},{["b"]="The riot laid bare the ugly underbelly of Stormwind's political machine. Outside territories had long grown frustrated with the House of Nobles halting executions for their own leverage. Redridge guard Berton wanted the captured orc Targorr the Dread executed for atrocities, only to watch nobles freeze the sentence. Darkshire councilman Millstipe watched the grave-robber Dextren Ward get spared the gallows despite desecrating graves for the necromancer Morbent Fel. Even the dwarves of Dun Modr sent word demanding the death of Dark Iron saboteur Kam Deepfury. With the Stockade in chaos, local authorities quietly hired adventurers to carry out the executions the capital's courts refused to enforce.",["t"]="Nobles and Assassins"},{["b"]="Behind the prison's synchronized collapse sits Bazil Thredd, a cunning felon tied directly to the Defias Brotherhood who orchestrated the uprising from his cell. Among the rioters is Bruegal Ironknuckle, a dangerous dwarven brawler. He is one of several hardened convicts adventurers may encounter while helping Thelwater reclaim the prison.",["t"]="The Mastermind and the Rioters"}}}}}
t["zone:stonetalon"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="20,410,27.610.0,22.630.0,17.537.0,41.251.0,42.215.0,52.258.0,42.193.0,54.257.0,36.219.0",["h"]="64ebca",["n"]="Sierra Espolón",["s"]="Sierra Espolón. Sierra Espolón es una escarpada cordillera de picos rocosos esculpidos por el viento que limita con Vallefresno, Los Baldíos y Desolace. Venerada tanto por los elfos de la noche como por los tauren como un santuario sagrado, las cumbres sagradas de la región contrastan fuertemente con el humo y la maquinaria que asfixian sus cañones inferiores. Hoy en día, las montañas están desgarradas por la deforestación, la devastación elemental volátil y las latentes hostilidades fronterizas. Elevándose abruptamente desde el centro de Kalimdor, la cordillera de Espolón atrapa la humedad que llega de los mares occidentales, dando lugar a repentinos aguaceros y traicioneros senderos de arenisca resbaladizos por la lluvia. El rasgo predominante de los valles bajos es el incesante vendaval abrasador conocido como los Vientos Aciagos, capaz de cegar a los viajeros con arenilla o de arrojar a los descuidados directamente por los escarpados acantilados. Más arriba, bosques de hoja perenne cubren las crestas que rodean Cima del Espolón, donde los elfos de la noche cuidan túmulos ancestrales y claros, vigilando los nidos sagrados de dracoleones e hipogrifos que anidan a lo largo del Lago Mirkfallon.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,0}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Druid"]="The desecration of Windshear Crag and the scorched corruption of the Charred Vale serve as a grave summons for druids seeking to soothe enraged nature spirits and protect sacred roosts.",["class:Shaman"]="The cry of the mountain spirits under assault by goblin drills can be heard as far as Orgrimmar, calling shamans to quell rampaging rock elementals and restore balance to the peaks.",["faction:Alliance"]="Stationed high atop Stonetalon Peak and in hidden camps around Windshear Crag, the Alliance defends ancient kaldorei barrows from goblin machines and encroaching territorial rivals.",["faction:Horde"]="Horde adventurers rely on the secluded refuge of Sun Rock Retreat and the southern troll post of Malaka'jin to curb Venture Company industrial pollution and root out treacherous Grimtotem outlaws."},["faq"]={{["a"]="Most travelers enter via the Talondeep Pass, a mountain trail cutting into the range from southwestern Ashenvale. There are also high trails leading south into Desolace and routes descending eastward directly into the northern expanses of the Barrens.",["al"]={"stonetalon entrance","how to get to stonetalon","stonetalon road"},["q"]="How do I get to the Stonetalon Mountains?"},{["a"]="The Charred Vale was once a lush haven, but cataclysmic devastation consumed the valley in flames. It has been reduced to a smoldering ruin crawling with aggressive fire elementals, rogue drakes, and deadly harpies.",["al"]={"charred vale fire","what happened to charred vale","why charred vale burnt"},["q"]="Why is the Charred Vale burned and ruined?"},{["a"]="The Venture Company is ruthlessly clear-cutting timber and drilling deep into the mountainside for mineral wealth. Their operations have fouled Cragpool Lake with engine grease, stripped the valleys bare, and enraged the local spirits and native inhabitants.",["al"]={"venture co stonetalon","windshear crag goblins","why are goblins cutting trees"},["q"]="What is the Venture Company doing in Windshear Crag?"},{["a"]="Alliance heroes use the flight master perched at the high sanctuary of Stonetalon Peak in the north, while Horde adventurers base themselves in the central mountain village of Sun Rock Retreat.",["al"]={"stonetalon flight path","where is flight point stonetalon","stonetalon fp"},["q"]="Where are the main flight masters located?"},{["a"]="Guided by visions of the Prophet Medivh, Thrall and Jaina Proudmoore climbed the caverns of Stonetalon Peak in search of the Oracle. There, the two faction leaders forged the historic temporary alliance that united against the Burning Legion at Mount Hyjal.",["al"]={"stonetalon peak lore","the oracle stonetalon","third war stonetalon"},["q"]="What happened at Stonetalon Peak during the Third War?"},{["a"]="The Grimtotem are a ruthless, renegade tauren tribe hostile to outsider influence. In the Stonetalons, they have occupied a mountain post near the border of the Barrens, terrorizing local travelers and taking prisoners.",["al"]={"grimtotem post","grimtotem stonetalon"},["q"]="Who are the Grimtotem in Stonetalon?"}},["kw"]={"charred vale","gerenzo wrenchwhistle","malaka'jin","stonetalon","stonetalon peak","sun rock retreat","talondeep pass","windshear"},["n"]="Stonetalon Mountains",["s"]="The Stonetalon Mountains are a rugged, wind-carved chain of craggy peaks bordering Ashenvale, the Barrens, and Desolace. Revered by both the night elves and the tauren as a sacred sanctuary, the region's sacred heights stand in sharp contrast to the smoke and machinery choking its lower canyons. Today, the mountains are torn by deforestation, volatile elemental devastation, and simmering border hostilities.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Rising sharply out of central Kalimdor, the Stonetalon range catches the moisture drifting from the western seas, giving birth to sudden downpours and treacherous, rain-slick sandstone trails. The dominant feature of the lower valleys is the incessant scouring gale known as the Ill Winds, capable of blinding travelers with grit or throwing the careless straight off sheer cliffsides. Higher up, evergreen forests mantle the ridges surrounding Stonetalon Peak, where the night elves tend ancestral barrows and glades, watching over the sacred roosts of wyverns and hippogryphs that nest along Mirkfallon Lake.",["t"]="The Ill Winds and the Peaks"},{["b"]="The peaceful solitude of the range has been violently disrupted by the arrival of the Venture Company. Setting up sprawling logging camps and digging deep strip mines in Windshear Crag, the goblins—aided by the deranged leper gnome Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle—have clear-cut old-growth timber, blackened the skies with furnace smoke, and poured industrial sludge into the mountain runoff. Their greed has provoked swift retaliation: local shamans from Orgrimmar answered the agonized cries of the mountain spirits, while small Alliance camps stationed nearby fight back against the defilers to protect what remains of the highlands.",["t"]="Industry and Intrusion"},{["b"]="To the south lies the Charred Vale, once a blossoming wilderness, now reduced to an ash-covered wasteland churning with rogue fire elementals, black dragonspawn, and harpies. While the peaks were the historic site where the Horde and Alliance forged an uneasy truce during the Third War under the guidance of the mysterious Oracle, current relations are fraying. Renegade Grimtotem tauren operate out of seized encampments, and Darkspear trolls in Malaka'jin clash with Alliance incursions pushing outward from the Barrens and Ashenvale.",["t"]="Scars of the Past"}}}}}
t["zone:stormwind"] = {["faq"]={{["a"]="El príncipe Anduin Wrynn, de diez años de edad, ostenta la corona, pero el gobierno real corre a cargo del Alto Señor Bolvar Fordragón, quien actúa como regente de Ventormenta y comandante supremo de sus fuerzas militares. Bolvar depende enormemente del consejo de la consejera real Lady Katrana Prestor mientras el Rey Varian Wrynn sigue desaparecido.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="69130d",["i"]=1,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="El Rey Varian desapareció en circunstancias sospechosas mientras viajaba en barco hacia una cumbre diplomática en Theramore. Su desaparición se mantuvo en secreto para preservar la estabilidad, aunque los rumores corren desbocados por las calles y el campo.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="0d4839",["i"]=2,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="La ciudad original fue reducida a cenizas por la Horda orca durante la Primera Guerra. Tras la Segunda Guerra, la Hermandad de los Albañiles, liderada por el maestro artesano Edwin VanCleef, reconstruyó minuciosamente la capital con reluciente piedra blanca gracias al respaldo financiero de la Alianza de Lordaeron.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="0db8b0",["i"]=3,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="La Casa de los Nobles acumuló cuantiosas deudas al expandir el ejército de Ventormenta y se negó a pagar a la Hermandad de los Albañiles el salario prometido. Cuando Edwin VanCleef exigió una compensación, los nobles disolvieron el gremio. Los trabajadores se amotinaron, la reina Tiffin Wrynn murió a causa de una pedrada y los canteros desterrados formaron la Hermandad Defias.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="93558e",["i"]=4,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Las estatuas honran a los Hijos de Lothar: el general Turalyon, la capitana forestal Alleria Brisaveloz, el archimago Khadgar, Kurdran Martillo Salvaje y Danath Aterratrols. Se erigieron para conmemorar a los héroes que se adentraron a través del Portal Oscuro hacia Draenor con el fin de sellar la fisura y proteger Azeroth.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="0190e7",["i"]=5,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Ventormenta se conecta con Forjaz mediante el Tren subterráneo, una vía ferroviaria subterránea construida por el Manitas Mayor Gelbin Mekkatorque y encargada por el Rey Magni Barbabronce para facilitar un comercio ágil y rápidos refuerzos militares entre ambas capitales.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="965943",["i"]=6,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="La corte real, fuertemente asesorada por Lady Katrana Prestor, insiste en que la ciudad no puede prescindir de tropas apostadas en sus propias murallas y fronteras inmediatas. Con la economía del reino al límite y las disputas políticas paralizando la Casa de los Nobles, los pueblos de la región han sido abandonados a su suerte, dependiendo de milicias locales.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="202320",["i"]=7,["q"]="",["sp"]=0}},["fp"]="14,461,19.657.0,15.605.0,27.577.0,19.516.2,30.299.0,35.231.0,38.286.0,48.333.0,50.279.0,40.240.0,65.292.0",["h"]="22c627",["n"]="Ciudad de Ventormenta",["s"]="Ciudad de Ventormenta. La Ciudad de Ventormenta es la bulliciosa capital del Reino de Ventormenta y el principal bastión humano en el mundo tras la caída de Lordaeron. Reconstruida de sus cenizas por la Hermandad de los Albañiles tras su destrucción en la Primera Guerra, sus magníficos distritos de piedra blanca albergan ahora el corazón de la Gran Alianza. No obstante, bajo sus majestuosos canales y catedrales, la ciudad lidia con la repentina desaparición del Rey Varian Wrynn y una creciente intriga política. Fundada hace más de un milenio por los descendientes de los Arathi, Ventormenta se mantuvo durante generaciones como un reino próspero y autosuficiente. Esa época dorada terminó cuando la Horda orca cruzó a raudales El Portal Oscuro, asolando el continente meridional y arrasando la capital por completo en la Primera Guerra. Los supervivientes, liderados por Sir Anduin Lothar, huyeron al norte a través del Mare Magnum hacia Lordaeron. Tras la derrota de la Horda en la Segunda Guerra, el Rey Terenas Menethil II y la Alianza financiaron una reconstrucción total. La Hermandad de los Albañiles reconstruyó minuciosamente la capital para convertirla en Nueva Ventormenta: una resplandeciente ciudadela de pulida piedra blanca y distritos entrelazados por canales.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,0,2}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Paladin"]="The Cathedral of Light serves as the supreme sanctuary of your faith in the south, training knights of the Silver Hand and upholding justice.",["class:Rogue"]="Beneath the pristine stone streets lies the SI:7 headquarters in Old Town, operating the Alliance's primary intelligence and espionage network.",["faction:Alliance"]="As the most populous metropolis within the Grand Alliance, Stormwind provides naval might, spiritual grounding, and vital diplomatic leadership.",["race:Dwarf"]="Through the Deeprun Tram, your kin in the Dwarven District maintain deep commercial ties and forge brotherly military bonds with the human kingdom.",["race:Human"]="Stormwind stands as your people's last great seat of sovereign power following the destruction of Lordaeron, Alterac, and Dalaran in the north."},["faq"]={{["a"]="Ten-year-old Prince Anduin Wrynn holds the crown, but actual governance is handled by Highlord Bolvar Fordragon, who acts as Regent of Stormwind and supreme commander of its military. Bolvar relies heavily on the counsel of royal advisor Lady Katrana Prestor while King Varian Wrynn remains missing.",["al"]={"current king","who is the king","who leads stormwind","king of stormwind"},["q"]="Who rules Stormwind right now?"},{["a"]="King Varian vanished under suspicious circumstances while traveling by ship to a diplomatic summit in Theramore. His disappearance was kept quiet to maintain stability, though rumors run rampant through the streets and countryside.",["al"]={"where is varian","missing king","varian gone","varian wrynn missing"},["q"]="What happened to King Varian Wrynn?"},{["a"]="The original city was burned to the ground by the Orcish Horde during the First War. After the Second War, the Stonemasons Guild, led by master craftsman Edwin VanCleef, painstakingly rebuilt the capital out of gleaming white stone with financial backing from the Alliance of Lordaeron.",["al"]={"who rebuilt stormwind","stonemasons guild","new stormwind","city rebuilding"},["q"]="Why was the city rebuilt, and by whom?"},{["a"]="The House of Nobles amassed heavy debts expanding Stormwind's military and refused to pay the Stonemasons Guild their promised wages. When Edwin VanCleef demanded restitution, the nobles disbanded the guild. The workers rioted, Queen Tiffin Wrynn was killed by a thrown rock, and the exiled stonemasons formed the Defias Brotherhood.",["al"]={"defias origin","why defias mad","stonemasons riot","queen tiffin death"},["q"]="Why did the stonemasons rebel against the crown?"},{["a"]="The statues honor the Sons of Lothar: General Turalyon, Ranger Captain Alleria Windrunner, Archmage Khadgar, Kurdran Wildhammer, and Danath Trollbane. They were raised to commemorate the heroes who ventured through the Dark Portal to draenor to seal the rift and protect Azeroth.",["al"]={"statues at the entrance","valley of heroes statues","sons of lothar statues"},["q"]="Who are the giant statues in the Valley of Heroes?"},{["a"]="Stormwind is connected to Ironforge by the Deeprun Tram, a subterranean railway built by High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque and commissioned by King Magni Bronzebeard to enable swift trade and rapid military reinforcement between both capitals.",["al"]={"deeprun tram","train to ironforge","how to get to ironforge"},["q"]="How does Stormwind connect to Ironforge?"},{["a"]="The royal court, heavily advised by Lady Katrana Prestor, insists the city cannot spare troops from its own walls and immediate borders. With the kingdom's finances stressed and political infighting paralyzing the House of Nobles, regional towns have been abandoned to rely on local militias.",["al"]={"stormwind army missing","why no help for westfall","reinforcements refused"},["q"]="Why isn't Stormwind sending troops to help Westfall and Redridge?"}},["kw"]={"anduin","bolvar","cathedral","deeprun","prestor","stonemasons","stormwind","vancleef","wrynn"},["n"]="Stormwind City",["s"]="Stormwind City is the bustling capital of the Kingdom of Stormwind and the premier human bastion in the world following the fall of Lordaeron. Rebuilt from ashes by the Stonemasons Guild after its destruction in the First War, its magnificent white stone districts now house the heart of the Grand Alliance. Beneath its majestic canals and cathedrals, however, the city grapples with the sudden disappearance of King Varian Wrynn and brewing political intrigue.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Founded over a millennium ago by the descendants of the Arathi, Stormwind stood for generations as a flourishing, self-sufficient realm. That golden era ended when the Orcish Horde poured through the Dark Portal, laying waste to the southern continent and razing the capital to the ground in the First War. Survivors led by Sir Anduin Lothar fled north across the Great Sea to Lordaeron. Following the Horde's defeat in the Second War, King Terenas Menethil II and the Alliance funded a complete reconstruction. The Stonemasons Guild painstakingly rebuilt the capital into New Stormwind—a gleaming citadel of polished white stone and canal-laced boroughs.",["t"]="Rise from the Ashes"},{["b"]="Despite its outward majesty, the capital is locked in domestic turmoil. A dispute between the House of Nobles and the Stonemasons Guild over wages left the kingdom paralyzed by debt. The ensuing rioting claimed the life of Queen Tiffin Wrynn and drove the exiled artisans into criminality as the Defias Brotherhood. Now, King Varian Wrynn has mysteriously vanished en route to a diplomatic summit. To avert panic, the crown was placed upon his young son, ten-year-old Prince Anduin Wrynn, with Highlord Bolvar Fordragon ruling as Regent and military commander alongside royal advisor Lady Katrana Prestor.",["t"]="A Divided Court"},{["b"]="With Lordaeron overrun by the Scourge, Stormwind has inherited the mantle of humanity's greatest haven and the epicenter of the Alliance. It connects directly to the dwarven kingdom of Ironforge via High Tinker Mekkatorque's subterranean Deeprun Tram. Visitors crossing the Valley of Heroes are greeted by towering statues of the Sons of Lothar who vanished beyond the Dark Portal. From the spiritual beacon of the Cathedral of Light to the arcane studies within the Wizard's Sanctum, Stormwind stands as the supreme hub of trade, culture, and defense across the southern seas.",["t"]="Heart of the Grand Alliance"},{["b"]="The discord plaguing the kingdom is no mere administrative accident. Lady Katrana Prestor has covertly guided the decisions of the House of Nobles, inflaming hostility with the stonemasons and willfully neglecting appeals for military relief from outlying settlements like Westfall and Redridge. By whispering poison into the ears of Highlord Bolvar and dividing the realm's attention, she works to keep Stormwind’s legions idle and divided while unrest festering beyond the city walls threatens the throne itself.",["t"]="Shadows in the Keep"}}}}}
t["zone:stranglethorn"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="18,435,18.552.0,22.509.0,22.494.0,25.463.1,32.373.0,18.360.0,32.325.1,18.354.0,32.393.0,38.317.0",["h"]="d13cbd",["n"]="Vega de Tuercespina",["s"]="Vega de Tuercespina. La Vega de Tuercespina es una jungla densa e indómita que domina el extremo sur de los Reinos del Este, al sur del Bosque del Ocaso. En su día sede del gran imperio Gurubashi, la selva tropical está ahora sembrada de ruinas trol derruidas, bestias salvajes y campamentos rivales de goblins, piratas y soldados. El control de la región se lo disputan con fiereza las tribus locales de trols, Ventura y Cía. y las facciones rivales que intentan afianzarse entre el calor. Durante milenios, el valle estuvo gobernado por el poderoso imperio Gurubashi desde su capital, Zul'Gurub. Ese dominio se hizo añicos cuando unos sacerdotes radicales invocaron a Hakkar el Cazador de Almas, sumiendo a la región en una catastrófica guerra civil. Aunque los Zandalari intervinieron para aplastar el alzamiento del dios de la sangre, el imperio quedó fragmentado sin remedio. Hoy en día, la selva está invadida por facciones sucesoras enfrentadas —destacando los Sangrapellejo en el noroeste y los Machacacráneos en el este—, y ambas masacran con celo a los forasteros para reclamar su hogar ancestral.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,1,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,0,1}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Hunter"]="The northern riverbank is home to the famed big-game hunter Hemet Nesingwary, who challenges marksmen to test their mettle tracking the jungle's fiercest raptors, panthers, and crocolisks.",["faction:Alliance"]="Stormwind's historical efforts to conquer and pacify the jungle saddled the kingdom with severe debt, and today its interests are defended only by hard-pressed mutineers at the Rebel Camp.",["faction:Horde"]="Grom'gol Base Camp offers the Horde an indispensable strategic stronghold on the southern continent, supplied directly by zeppelins from Durotar and the Undercity.",["profession:Herbalism"]="The humid canopy nourishes valuable flora, making the riverbanks and coastlines ideal hunting grounds for Wild Steelbloom, Fadeleaf, Goldthorn, and Purple Lotus.",["race:Troll"]="Stranglethorn is the ancestral cradle of the jungle trolls, whose ancient Gurubashi empire fell to civil war after worshipping Hakkar the Soulflayer, driving tribes like the Darkspear across the seas."},["faq"]={{["a"]="No single power rules the vale. The jungle is divided among rival jungle troll tribes like the Bloodscalps and Skullsplitters, rogue encampments like Kurzen's Compound, the mercenary Venture Company, and scattered Horde and Alliance military posts. The largest urban settlement is the neutral goblin port of Booty Bay, governed by Baron Revilgaz and the Blackwater Raiders.",["al"]={"who owns stranglethorn","who rules stv","stranglethorn factions"},["q"]="Who controls Stranglethorn Vale?"},{["a"]="Booty Bay is a sprawling port city built on the southern bluffs of the Cape of Stranglethorn. Originally a human settlement known as Blackwater Cove, it was captured by trolls and eventually secured by the Steamwheedle Cartel. Today, Baron Revilgaz and Fleet Master Seahorn run it as a neutral trading port connecting travelers to Ratchet across the Great Sea.",["al"]={"what is booty bay","booty bay port","who runs booty bay"},["q"]="What is Booty Bay?"},{["a"]="Colonel Kurzen led an expedition into the jungle on behalf of Stormwind, but his mind fell under the sway of Mai'Zoth, an ogre mage wielding a powerful relic called the Mind's Eye. Kurzen became increasingly erratic and despotic, forcing loyal soldiers like Lieutenant Doren to break away and form the Rebel Camp to stop him.",["al"]={"why is kurzen evil","what happened to kurzen","kurzen backstory"},["q"]="Why did Colonel Kurzen go rogue?"},{["a"]="Zul'Gurub is the ancient, temple-studded capital city of the Gurubashi Empire nestled in northeastern Stranglethorn. It was the epicenter of the civil war that ended the empire fifteen centuries ago, after radical priests summoned the blood god Hakkar the Soulflayer. Though long abandoned to vines and ruins, dark rituals continue to threaten its halls.",["al"]={"what is zg","zul gurub history","capital of gurubashi"},["q"]="What is Zul'Gurub?"},{["a"]="The Stone of the Tides is a legendary relic from ancient Gurubashi history discovered on the coast by a troll warrior. It granted its bearer dominion over water and elemental summons akin to a powerful mage, earning the wielder the title 'Tidebearer'. However, it bore a curse that caused the wielder to slowly fade from the physical realm, leading each bearer to walk into the sea and vanish.",["al"]={"stone of the tides","tidebearer legend","gurubashi water stone"},["q"]="What was the Stone of the Tides?"},{["a"]="Travelers can walk south across the river border from Duskwood into northern Stranglethorn. The Horde maintains direct zeppelin towers connecting Grom'gol Base Camp to Durotar and Tirisfal Glades. Travelers from Kalimdor can also board the regular merchant vessel sailing between Ratchet in the Barrens and Booty Bay.",["al"]={"how to get to stv","getting to stranglethorn","stv travel"},["q"]="How do I travel to Stranglethorn Vale?"}},["kw"]={"bloodsail","booty bay","grom'gol","gurubashi","kurzen","nesingwary","revilgaz","stranglethorn","venture co","zul'gurub"},["n"]="Stranglethorn Vale",["s"]="Stranglethorn Vale is an untamed, dense jungle dominating the southern tip of the Eastern Kingdoms, south of Duskwood. Once the seat of the great Gurubashi Empire, the rainforest is now strewn with crumbling troll ruins, savage beasts, and competing encampments of goblins, pirates, and soldiers. Control of the region is hotly contested by local troll tribes, the Venture Company, and rival factions carving out footholds in the heat.",["sec"]={{["b"]="For millennia, the vale was governed by the mighty Gurubashi Empire from their capital city, Zul'Gurub. That dominion shattered when radical priests summoned Hakkar the Soulflayer, plunging the region into a catastrophic civil war. Though the Zandalari stepped in to crush the blood god's uprising, the empire splintered beyond repair. Today, the jungle is overrun by warring successor factions—most prominently the Bloodscalp in the northwest and the Skullsplitter in the east—both zealously slaughtering outsiders to reclaim their ancestral home.",["t"]="Ruins of an Empire"},{["b"]="Where ancient ruins fall silent, commercial greed fills the void. At the southern tip lies Booty Bay, a bustling haven carved out by the Steamwheedle Cartel and run by the Blackwater Raiders under Baron Revilgaz. The port serves as the prime hub of southern commerce and seafaring transport, though it is perpetually besieged by the rival Bloodsail Buccaneers anchored along the coast. Further inland, the Venture Company aggressively strips the jungle of timber and mines the hills for rare singing crystals.",["t"]="Pirates and Profiteers"},{["b"]="Stranglethorn Vale serves as one of the most volatile borderlands in the world. The Horde commands Grom'gol Base Camp on the western coast, tethered by goblin zeppelins to Durotar and Tirisfal Glades, while Alliance loyalists operate out of the Rebel Camp to track down the rogue Colonel Kurzen. Amid the dense foliage, the legendary hunter Hemet Nesingwary leads daring big-game expeditions, drawing wanderers into perilous encounters with prowling panthers, enormous apes, and enemy soldiers.",["t"]="A Contested Wilderness"},{["b"]="Not all threats in northern Stranglethorn are wild beasts. Colonel Kurzen, once a decorated commander dispatched by Stormwind, went mad within the brush and established a tyrannical mercenary redoubt. In truth, Kurzen's mind was ensnared through the sorcery of the Mind's Eye, an artifact wielded by the ogre mage Mai'Zoth of the nearby Mosh'Ogg Ogre Mound, driving the expedition into utter insanity and prompting his former men to form the breakaway Rebel Camp.",["t"]="The Madness in the Jungle"}}}}}
t["zone:swampofsorrows"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="16,454,17.720.0,26.592.0,16.542.0,36.265.0,44.301.0,16.273.0,40.245.0,51.306.0,19.225.0",["h"]="26f4f8",["n"]="Pantano de las Penas",["s"]="Pantano de las Penas. El Pantano de las Penas es un humedal turbio y salobre encajonado entre el Paso de la Muerte y las áridas Tierras Devastadas. Antaño la extensión septentrional de la antigua Ciénaga Negra, sus cenagales cubiertos de costras de sal albergan una fauna peligrosa, dragonantes verdes y las ruinas del Templo de Atal'Hakkar sumergidas bajo la Charca de Lágrimas. Aunque la Horda mantiene la guarnición fortificada de Rocal, la Alianza carece de refugio seguro en la ciénaga, lo que deja estas tierras salvajes en gran medida indómitas. Mucho antes de la llegada de la Horda orca, estas marismas formaban la mitad norte de un enorme humedal conocido como la Ciénaga Negra. Tras la caída del Imperio Gurubashi, los fanáticos trols Atal'ai huyeron a la ciénaga para erigir el Templo de Atal'Hakkar con la intención de invocar a su sanguinario loa, Hakkar el Cazador de Almas. Al presentir la catástrofe, el Aspecto Ysera desató sus poderes y ahogó la pirámide maldita bajo las aguas de la Charca de Lágrimas. Se apostaron guardianes del Vuelo Verde liderados por Eranikus para vigilar con celo la estructura anegada. Cuando el cataclismo del Portal Oscuro calcinó más tarde el sur del pantano convirtiéndolo en Las Tierras Devastadas, este cenagal septentrional recibió su lamentable nombre actual.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,0}},["text"]={["ang"]={["faction:Alliance"]="Without an outpost to rally behind, traveling through the swamp is an isolated, hostile venture requiring careful preparation from Darkshire or Nethergarde Keep.",["faction:Horde"]="Stonard serves as your vital forward redoubt in the southern continent, hosting direct wyvern routes to Kargath, Flame Crest, and Grom'gol Base Camp.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Tough adventurers can gather iron, gold, mithril, and truesilver along the mountainous crags framing the swamp's northern and southern edges.",["profession:Herbalism"]="The marsh is one of the few places in Azeroth rich in blindweed, alongside generous growths of liferoot, fadeleaf, and stranglekelp.",["race:Troll"]="The sunken ruins of Atal'Hakkar stand as a dire reminder of the dark blood magic that fractured the Gurubashi Empire and drew the wrath of the Dragon Aspects."},["faq"]={{["a"]="The most common overland route leads east from Darkshire in Duskwood, through the perilous haunted defile of Deadwind Pass, and straight down into the marsh. Alternatively, travelers coming from the south can walk north along the main road out of the Blasted Lands.",["al"]={"how to get to swamp of sorrows","swamp of sorrows road","getting to stonard"},["q"]="How do I reach the Swamp of Sorrows?"},{["a"]="Centuries ago, the Atal'ai priesthood tried to summon Hakkar the Soulflayer into Azeroth. Ysera, the Dragon Aspect of the Green Flight, discovered their demonic blood rites and drowned the entire temple into the bog to avert the disaster, leaving behind green dragonspawn to guard the submerged halls.",["al"]={"why is sunken temple sunk","who sank the temple","sunken temple lore"},["q"]="Why is the Temple of Atal'Hakkar underwater?"},{["a"]="Stonard was one of the first settlements built by the Orcish Horde after crossing through the Dark Portal during the First War. Though ravaged during the fighting, it was recently reclaimed and rebuilt under Thrall's orders to anchor Horde logistics near the Blasted Lands.",["al"]={"stonard history","orc base in swamp","who built stonard"},["q"]="What is Stonard?"},{["a"]="No. The Alliance maintains no towns, guards, or flight paths in the Swamp of Sorrows. Alliance adventurers operating in the area must base themselves out of Darkshire in Duskwood to the west or Nethergarde Keep in the Blasted Lands to the south.",["al"]={"alliance base swamp of sorrows","alliance flight path swamp","where is alliance camp"},["q"]="Are there any Alliance settlements here?"},{["a"]="They are draenei who were stranded on Azeroth following the collapse of the Dark Portal. Severed from their world, most went mad from despair and turned into hostile Lost Ones. A small pocket of sane Broken exiles took refuge in the northwest at the Harborage, choosing neutral survival over mindless rage.",["al"]={"who are the lost ones","what is the harborage","draenei in swamp"},["q"]="Who are the strange mutated humanoids in the swamp?"},{["a"]="Sorrowmurk is the dense tract of marshland directly east of the Pool of Tears. It serves as a patrol ground for elite green dragonspawn and whelps who violently turn away mortals to safeguard the secrets of the Sunken Temple.",["al"]={"dragon area swamp of sorrows","sorrowmurk dragons","green dragonspawn swamp"},["q"]="What is Sorrowmurk?"}},["kw"]={"black morass","broken","harborage","lost ones","pool of tears","sorrowmurk","stonard","sunken temple","swamp of sorrows","temple of atal'hakkar"},["n"]="Swamp of Sorrows",["s"]="The Swamp of Sorrows is a murky, brackish wetland tucked between Deadwind Pass and the arid Blasted Lands. Once the northern expanse of the ancient Black Morass, its salt-crusted bogs are home to dangerous wildlife, green dragonspawn, and the ruined Temple of Atal'Hakkar sunken beneath the Pool of Tears. While the Horde maintains the fortified garrison of Stonard, the Alliance has no safe haven within the mire, leaving the wilderness largely untamed.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Long before the coming of the Orcish Horde, these marshes formed the northern half of an enormous wetlands known as the Black Morass. Following the shattering of the Gurubashi Empire, the fanatical Atal'ai trolls fled into the mire to erect the Temple of Atal'Hakkar, intending to summon their bloodthirsty loa, Hakkar the Soulflayer. Sensing the catastrophe, the Aspect Ysera unleashed her powers, drowning the cursed pyramid beneath the waters of the Pool of Tears. Green dragonflight guardians led by Eranikus were stationed to vigilantly watch over the drowned structure. When the Dark Portal's cataclysm later scorched the southern morass into the Blasted Lands, this northern fen earned its current sorrowful name.",["t"]="A Drowned History"},{["b"]="Stonard serves as the primary civilized foothold in the swamp. Originally established during the First War alongside the now-ruined settlements of Rockard and Kyross, the outpost was rebuilt under Thrall to serve as the Horde's southernmost staging ground in the Eastern Kingdoms. Beyond Stonard's palisades, strange wanderers roam the fen. A group of draenei stragglers stranded on Azeroth succumbed to homesickness and madness, degenerating into feral Lost Ones. However, a small enclave of lucid Broken exiles persists at the Harborage, wary but willing to trade peacefully with travelers.",["t"]="Outposts and Broken Exiles"},{["b"]="Surviving the swamp requires constant vigilance against its predatory wildlife. Deep bogs hide lunging crocolisks, venomous spiders, prowling swamp jaguars, and wandering bog beasts encrusted with marsh debris. Coastal caverns and the Misty Reed Strand crawl with vicious murlocs, while the salt-laden tide creeps continually inland, preserving rotten logs in thick briny crusts. Farther east lies Sorrowmurk, where aggressive elite dragonspawn fiercely guard the approaches to the Sunken Temple, attacking anyone who strays near their vigil.",["t"]="The Sinking Mire"}}}}}
t["zone:teldrassil"] = {["faq"]={{["a"]="Tras la devastación de Nordrassil al final de la Tercera Guerra, los elfos de la noche perdieron su inmortalidad. El archidruida Fandral Corzocelada instó al Círculo de los Ancestros y a los druidas del Círculo Cenarion a plantar un nuevo Árbol del Mundo en el mar, con la esperanza de recuperar lo que su pueblo había perdido.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="efcbd2",["i"]=1,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Nordrassil otorgaba originalmente la inmortalidad gracias a la bendición de Nozdormu, el Atemporal. Cuando se alzó Teldrassil, Nozdormu consideró que el deseo de los elfos de la noche era arrogante y se negó a conceder su bendición al nuevo árbol.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="4fa4be",["i"]=2,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Los viajeros deben dirigirse a través de Darnassus hacia el portal rosa de Los Jardines del Templo, que los transporta a la Aldea Rut'theran, en la base del tronco. Desde Rut'theran, un barco navega a través del mar hacia Auberdine, en la costa de Costa Oscura, además de haber rutas de hipogrifos disponibles.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="8606e3",["i"]=3,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Toda la zona se asienta sobre las ramas vivas y la madera de un descomunal Árbol del Mundo, en lugar de roca continental natural. Al no existir una corteza subterránea pétrea, no se forman vetas de metal ni yacimientos de mineral, aunque las hierbas florecen en gran abundancia.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="3078e5",["i"]=4,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Una siniestra presencia ha comenzado a filtrarse en las raíces no bendecidas de Teldrassil. La enfermedad ha corrompido el agua y la flora locales, empujando a los pacíficos maderizos y a la tribu fúrbolg Tuercepinos a una agresión frenética, mientras los reductos de sátiros cultivan activamente la corrupción.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="b6c98e",["i"]=5,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Cañada Umbría es un valle resguardado y apartado en las ramas orientales de Teldrassil que rodea al gran árbol Aldrassil. Sirve como un santuario pacífico donde los jóvenes elfos de la noche comienzan su instrucción antes de aventurarse en el resto del mundo.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="e4b484",["i"]=6,["q"]="",["sp"]=0}},["fp"]="10,340,21.456.0,26.478.0,17.517.1,25.261.0,52.223.0,28.276.0,44.256.0,52.264.0,19.232.0",["h"]="d47e75",["n"]="Teldrassil",["s"]="Teldrassil. Teldrassil, que significa «Corona de la Tierra», es un nuevo y gigantesco Árbol del Mundo alzado frente a la costa noroeste de Kalimdor. Creado por los druidas tras la Tercera Guerra, sus colosales ramas albergan una vasta naturaleza en penumbra y la capital de los elfos de la noche, Darnassus. Sin embargo, bajo su apacible fronda iluminada por la luna, ha comenzado a agitarse una insidiosa corrupción. Teldrassil no es un simple árbol; es un ecosistema elevado que se alza majestuoso en las aguas del Mar Velado. Sus gigantescas ramas sostienen colinas onduladas, ríos alimentados por el rocío matinal, lagos brumosos y densos claros ancestrales. En su extremo occidental se encuentra Darnassus, la gran capital de los kaldorei. En la base de su monumental tronco descansa la Aldea Rut'theran, conectada con la copa superior mediante un portal místico y convertida en el puerto principal de la isla.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,1}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Druid"]="With Malfurion trapped in the Emerald Dream, Archdruid Fandral Staghelm leads the Cenarion Circle here, though many druids worry that Teldrassil was raised in haste.",["faction:Alliance"]="Teldrassil serves as the northernmost bastion of the Alliance in Kalimdor, anchoring naval communication and trade between Darnassus and mainland kingdoms.",["profession:Herbalism"]="Lacking minerals and ore entirely, the tree's verdant canopy makes up for it by acting as a paradise for gatherers of Peacebloom, Silverleaf, and Earthroot.",["race:NightElf"]="This massive tree is your people's fresh beginning after the devastation of Mount Hyjal, but the absence of the Aspects' blessings makes defending it an urgent duty."},["faq"]={{["a"]="After Nordrassil was devastated at the end of the Third War, the night elves lost their immortality. Archdruid Fandral Staghelm pushed the Circle of Ancients and Cenarion druids to plant a new World Tree in the sea, aiming to restore what their people had lost.",["al"]={"why made","who made teldrassil","teldrassil origin"},["q"]="Why was Teldrassil grown?"},{["a"]="Nordrassil originally granted immortality because Nozdormu, the Timeless One, blessed it. When Teldrassil was raised, Nozdormu judged the night elves' desire as arrogant and refused to bestow his blessing upon the new tree.",["al"]={"immortality","why not immortal","nozdormu blessing"},["q"]="Why didn't Teldrassil restore night elf immortality?"},{["a"]="Travelers must head through Darnassus to the pink portal in the Temple Gardens, which transports them down to Rut'theran Village at the trunk's base. From Rut'theran, a ship sails across the sea to Auberdine on the mainland of Darkshore, alongside available hippogryph routes.",["al"]={"how to get out","leave tree","teldrassil boat"},["q"]="How do you leave Teldrassil?"},{["a"]="The entire zone is situated atop the living branches and wood of a colossal World Tree rather than natural continental rock. Because there are no stony subterranean crusts, no metal veins or ore deposits form here, though herbs flourish in great abundance.",["al"]={"where is copper","no ore","teldrassil mining"},["q"]="Why are there no mining nodes in Teldrassil?"},{["a"]="A sinister presence has begun to seep into Teldrassil's unblessed roots. Sickness has corrupted the local water and flora, driving peaceful timberlings and the Gnarlpine furbolg tribe into frenzied aggression, while remnants of satyrs actively cultivate the taint.",["al"]={"corrupted furbolgs","gnarlpine mad","what is the corruption"},["q"]="What is causing the furbolgs and beasts to turn mad?"},{["a"]="Shadowglen is a sheltered, secluded vale on the eastern boughs of Teldrassil surrounding the great tree Aldrassil. It serves as a peaceful sanctuary where young night elves begin their training before venturing into the wider world.",["al"]={"shadowglen","starting area","aldrassil zone"},["q"]="What is Shadowglen?"}},["kw"]={"circle of ancients","crown of the earth","darnassus","dolanaar","fandral staghelm","rut'theran","shadowglen","teldrassil","world tree"},["n"]="Teldrassil",["s"]="Teldrassil, meaning 'Crown of the Earth', is a gargantuan new World Tree raised off Kalimdor's northwest coast. Grown by the druids in the wake of the Third War, its colossal boughs support an entire twilit wilderness and the night elf capital of Darnassus. Yet beneath its tranquil, moonlit canopy, a creeping corruption has begun to stir.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Teldrassil is not merely a tree; it is an elevated ecosystem standing tall amidst the waters of the Veiled Sea. Its gargantuan limbs support rolling hills, flowing rivers fed by morning dew, misty lakes, and dense ancient glades. At its western edge sits Darnassus, the grand capital of the kaldorei. At the base of its monumental trunk lies Rut'theran Village, connected to the crown above by a mystical gateway and serving as the island's primary harbor.",["t"]="A Canopy Upon the Sea"},{["b"]="Following the sacrifice of Nordrassil during the Third War and the mysterious disappearance of Shan'do Malfurion Stormrage into the Emerald Dream, Archdruid Fandral Staghelm convinced the Circle of Ancients to grow a replacement. Staghelm hoped to restore the kaldorei to their lost immortality. However, Nozdormu and the Dragon Aspects deemed the effort hubristic and withheld their blessings. Without their vital warding, Teldrassil's growth has suffered from unnatural flaws.",["t"]="The Refusal of the Aspects"},{["b"]="Despite its outward majesty, the nascent World Tree harbors a dark sickness. The local wildlife has turned violent: peaceful furbolg tribes have fallen into madness, twisted grells multiply in the barrows, and vile satyrs work dark rites in shadows like Fel Rock. Down upon the shores of Darkshore, sea giants, threshers, and turtles beach themselves to flee the tainted waters around the tree. Naturalists and sentinels now labor frantically across the boughs to identify the shadow slowly strangling their new home.",["t"]="A Creeping Malady"}}}}}
t["zone:thousandneedles"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="16,315,17.605.0,18.606.0,18.558.0,38.246.0,59.275.0,43.301.0,27.259.0,45.264.0,30.290.0",["h"]="bef081",["n"]="Las Mil Agujas",["s"]="Las Mil Agujas. Las Mil Agujas es un extenso cañón de escarpados pilares de arenisca y salinas abrasadas por el sol en el sur de Kalimdor. Las tribus tauren se aferran a asentamientos en lo alto de imponentes mesetas para escapar de las agresivas manadas de centauros, mientras que manitas gnomos y goblins conducen coches cohete de alta velocidad por el lecho marino seco de El Desierto de Sal. Antes de que el Gran Cataclismo destrozara el antiguo Kalimdor, este territorio era un frondoso cañón fluvial alimentado por vías fluviales interiores que desembocaban en el mar. El cataclismo desgarró el continente, elevando el lecho del río por encima del nivel freático y alterando su curso hasta que solo quedaron piedra seca y agujas de arenisca. Hoy en día, decenas de esbeltas columnas cilíndricas se alzan desde el fondo del cañón como un bosque petrificado. Los tauren se asentaron en las cumbres, tendiendo precarios puentes de cuerda entre los picos para establecer aldeas fuera del alcance de los hostiles merodeadores centauros que patrullan el sombrío fondo del cañón.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,0}},["text"]={["ang"]={["faction:Horde"]="The Great Lift links the Barrens to your stronghold at Freewind Post, serving as a strategic southern boundary against centaur clans and rebel tauren.",["profession:Engineering"]="The salt flats are a pilgrimage site for tinkerers looking to study rocket fuel, vehicle parts, and high-speed propulsion at the Mirage Raceway.",["race:Gnome"]="The Mirage Raceway in the Shimmering Flats is your faction's premier chance to demonstrate the precision and elegance of gnomish engineering over shoddy goblin contraptions.",["race:Tauren"]="Freewind Post stands as a reminder of how high your people had to climb to escape centaur slaughter, and a staging ground against the treacherous Grimtotem at Darkcloud Pinnacle."},["faq"]={{["a"]="The zone is named after the dozens of massive, cylindrical sandstone spires that erupt from the canyon bottom. Originally carved by rushing river currents before the Great Sundering, these geological towers now resemble a forest of giant needles.",["al"]={"why is it called thousand needles","needle pillars origin","spires lore"},["q"]="How did Thousand Needles get its name?"},{["a"]="Travelers take the Great Lift, an immense double-car elevator operated by Horde guards on the southern border of the Barrens. Stepping off the edge is fatal, so waiting for the platform is essential. Alliance travelers can also hike in from the west via Thalanaar in Feralas.",["al"]={"how to get to thousand needles","great lift entrance","elevator to needles"},["q"]="How do you get down into Thousand Needles from the Barrens?"},{["a"]="Centaur warbands once pushed the nomadic tauren nearly to extinction. Building outposts like Freewind Post atop inaccessible stone pillars kept them safe from ground charges. They connected the mesas using wooden suspension bridges, allowing high-altitude travel above the predators and raiders below.",["al"]={"why are tauren on pillars","freewind post history","tauren high ground"},["q"]="Why do the tauren live on top of the mesas?"},{["a"]="The Mirage Raceway is an engineering proving ground on the Shimmering Flats salt pan. Gnomish and goblin tinkerers run experimental rocket-propelled racecars across the flat salt bed to settle their ongoing rivalry over which race builds the finest machinery.",["al"]={"salt flats track","gnome goblin race","rocket car track"},["q"]="What is the Mirage Raceway?"},{["a"]="The Grimtotem clan occupies Darkcloud Pinnacle, a fortified cluster of spires directly opposing Freewind Post. Unlike the rest of the tauren tribes who pledged to the Horde, the Grimtotem actively attack other tauren and conspire to seize dominance over the mesas.",["al"]={"grimtotem thousand needles","darkcloud pinnacle tauren","evil tauren needles"},["q"]="What is the Grimtotem presence in the canyon?"},{["a"]="The Shimmering Flats are a vast, dried lakebed east of the canyon mesas. The salt crust formed when the ancient inland sea evaporated after the Sundering. The searing heat, blinding white salt, and harsh predators make it an inhospitable desert used mainly for high-speed vehicular testing.",["al"]={"salt pan","white desert needles","eastern desert"},["q"]="What are the Shimmering Flats?"}},["kw"]={"cliffwatcher longhorn","darkcloud pinnacle","fizzle brassbolts","freewind post","great lift","grimtotem","kravel koalbeard","mirage raceway","pozzik","shimmering flats","thousand needles"},["n"]="Thousand Needles",["s"]="Thousand Needles is a sprawling canyon of sheer sandstone pillars and sunbaked salt pans in southern Kalimdor. Tauren tribes cling to settlements atop towering mesas to escape aggressive centaur packs, while gnomish and goblin tinkerers run high-speed rocket cars across the dried-up seabed of the Shimmering Flats.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Before the Great Sundering shattered ancient Kalimdor, this territory was a lush river canyon fed by inland waterways flowing out toward the sea. The cataclysm tore the continent apart, thrusting the riverbed above the water table and altering the river's path until only dry stone and sandstone spires remained. Today, dozens of slender cylindrical columns rise from the canyon floor like a petrified forest. Tauren settled on the summits, stringing precarious rope bridges between peaks to establish villages out of reach of the hostile centaur marauders patrolling the shadow-draped canyon floor below.",["t"]="A Forest of Stone"},{["b"]="Atop the needle-like spires, life is far from peaceful. At Freewind Post, tauren loyal to the Horde contend not only with predatory wind serpents and wyverns from Highperch, but also with their treacherous kin: the militant Grimtotem clan entrenched at Darkcloud Pinnacle. Cliffwatcher Longhorn oversees the defense of the plateau, seeking bold fighters to strike at Grimtotem agitators and secure the fragile bridges linking the peaks. To the north, the enormous mechanical winches of the Great Lift carry travelers down from the southern Barrens, though crossing the canyon floor remains a deadly gambit.",["t"]="Peril on the Mesas"},{["b"]="To the southeast, the canyon walls peel back into the Shimmering Flats, an enormous, desiccated salt pan sitting below sea level. In this harsh, blinding white expanse, gnomes and goblins maintain an uneasy neutrality around the Mirage Raceway. Rival engineers Pozzik and Fizzle Brassbolts race experimental rocket cars across the hard salt to settle which race possesses technological superiority. The pit stop attracts scoundrels, swindlers like Kravel Koalbeard, and hunters hunting the hardy scorpions, turtles, and basilisks that roam the searing basin.",["t"]="The Mirage Raceway"}}}}}
t["zone:thunderbluff"] = {["faq"]={{["a"]="El Gran Jefe Cairne Pezuña de Sangre gobierna Cima del Trueno y lidera a las tribus tauren unidas. Venerado por su sabiduría, paciencia y colosal fuerza, Cairne fundó la ciudad tras aliarse con la Horda de Thrall y expulsar a los centauros de Mulgore.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="88e03e",["i"]=1,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Los visitantes que llegan a pie o a lomos de un kodo deben montar en inmensos elevadores de plataformas de madera situados en los lados suroeste y noreste de la meseta central. Como alternativa, la ciudad cuenta con un nido de jinetes del viento con rutas de vuelo por todo Kalimdor.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="7ba860",["i"]=2,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Los siglos de existencia nómada estuvieron marcados por implacables ataques relámpago de agresivos clanes centauros. Los escarpados acantilados de los riscos de Mulgore proporcionan una defensa natural, protegiendo las viviendas, forjas y ancianos sagrados de los tauren de las emboscadas terrestres y permitiéndoles asentarse permanentemente en paz.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="540aaa",["i"]=3,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="La ciudad se extiende por una meseta central (dividida en niveles inferior, medio y superior) rodeada por tres mesetas exteriores. El Alto de los Cazadores está dedicado a guerreros, cazadores y pabellones de rastreo. El Alto de los Ancestros es el hogar de druidas, sabios y la facción Tótem Siniestro. El Alto de los Espíritus alberga santuarios dedicados a la Madre Tierra, instructores de chamanes, sacerdotes y magos, y las cavernosas Pozas de las Visiones.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="56aacf",["i"]=4,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Las Pozas de las Visiones son cavernas subterráneas sagradas ocultas bajo el Alto de los Espíritus. Videntes tauren, caminantes espirituales y practicantes de lo arcano se reúnen en estas aguas tranquilas y envueltas en niebla para comulgar con los elementos e interpretar presagios sobre el futuro de su pueblo.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="5b3399",["i"]=5,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Magatha Tótem Siniestro es la matriarca y Vieja Bruja del formidable clan Tótem Siniestro. Aunque reside en el Alto de los Ancestros y participa aparentemente en el consejo de Cairne, es ferozmente tradicional, desconfía de los forasteros y desafía en secreto la postura tolerante de la tribu Pezuña de Sangre hacia la Horda.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="b1a11f",["i"]=6,["q"]="",["sp"]=0}},["fp"]="13,473,17.558.0,20.575.0,12.516.1,24.238.0,35.236.0,61.300.0,41.364.0,29.270.0,25.291.0",["h"]="67d371",["n"]="Cima del Trueno",["s"]="Cima del Trueno. Cima del Trueno es la magnífica capital de los tauren, erigida en lo alto de mesetas de roca roja azotadas por el viento que se alzan sobre las praderas de Mulgore. Fundada por el Gran Jefe Cairne Pezuña de Sangre tras unir a las tribus nómadas con la ayuda de la Nueva Horda, sirve como corazón cultural, espiritual y mercantil de su pueblo. Conectada por puentes colgantes de cuerda y elevadores de madera, la ciudad ofrece protección frente a los merodeadores centauros y un santuario pacífico para los viajeros de todo Kalimdor. Durante incontables generaciones, los tauren vivieron como nómadas errantes por Kalimdor, enfrascados en una brutal lucha por la supervivencia contra voraces tribus de centauros. Esa era desesperada llegó a su fin durante la Tercera Guerra, cuando Cairne Pezuña de Sangre unió fuerzas con el Jefe de Guerra Thrall. Juntos, orcos y tauren repelieron los asaltos de los centauros y aseguraron los exuberantes pastos de Mulgore. Para proteger su futuro, Cairne condujo a las tribus aliadas a la seguridad de estos bastiones naturales de piedra, fundando Cima del Trueno como el primer hogar unificado y permanente que los tauren habían tenido en siglos.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,1}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Druid"]="Elder Rise serves as the Horde's supreme sanctuary of nature magic, headed by Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem, the first tauren druid taught in thousands of years.",["class:Shaman"]="Spirit Rise and the subterranean Pools of Vision provide a serene nexus where the elemental spirits and spirits of tauren ancestors are revered and heard.",["faction:Horde"]="Far more tranquil and spiritual than the harsh battlements of Orgrimmar, Thunder Bluff serves as the pastoral anchor of the New Horde's southern influence in Kalimdor.",["race:Tauren"]="Thunder Bluff represents the hard-won unity and rebirth of your people after generations of nomadic wandering and centaur harassment under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof."},["faq"]={{["a"]="High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof rules Thunder Bluff and leads the united tauren tribes. Revered for his wisdom, patience, and mighty strength, Cairne founded the city after allying with Thrall's Horde and driving the centaur from Mulgore.",["al"]={"leader of thunder bluff","who is in charge","thunder bluff chieftain","cairne"},["q"]="Who rules Thunder Bluff?"},{["a"]="Visitors arriving on foot or kodo must ride massive wooden platform elevators located on the southwestern and northeastern sides of the central mesa. Alternatively, the city features a Wind Rider roost with flight paths across Kalimdor.",["al"]={"thunder bluff elevator","how to get in","thunder bluff entrance","getting up"},["q"]="How do you get up to Thunder Bluff?"},{["a"]="Centuries of nomadic existence were marked by relentless hit-and-run attacks from aggressive centaur clans. The sheer cliffs of Mulgore's bluffs provide natural defense, shielding the tauren's dwellings, smiths, and sacred elders from ground ambushes and allowing them to permanently settle in peace.",["al"]={"why mesas","why are they on cliffs","thunder bluff cliffs","tauren high ground"},["q"]="Why did the tauren build on high mesas instead of the ground?"},{["a"]="The city spans a central mesa (divided into lower, middle, and high tiers) surrounded by three outer mesas. Hunter Rise is dedicated to warriors, hunters, and tracking lodges. Elder Rise is home to druids, wisemen, and the Grimtotem faction. Spirit Rise holds shrines to the Earth Mother, trainers of shamans, priests, and mages, and the cavernous Pools of Vision.",["al"]={"thunder bluff areas","what are the rises","districts of thunder bluff"},["q"]="What are the different rises of the city?"},{["a"]="The Pools of Vision are sacred underground caverns tucked beneath Spirit Rise. Tauren seers, spiritwalkers, and practitioners of the arcane gather in these quiet, mist-shrouded waters to commune with the elements and interpret omens regarding the future of their people.",["al"]={"pools of vision","spirit rise cave","thunder bluff cave"},["q"]="What are the Pools of Vision?"},{["a"]="Magatha Grimtotem is the matriarch and Elder Crone of the formidable Grimtotem clan. While she resides on Elder Rise and outwardly participates in Cairne's council, she is fiercely traditional, distrusting outsiders and quietly challenging the Bloodhoof tribe's soft stance toward the Horde.",["al"]={"magatha","elder crone","who is the grimtotem leader"},["q"]="Who is Magatha Grimtotem?"}},["kw"]={"cairne bloodhoof","elder rise","elevators","hamuul runetotem","hunter rise","magatha grimtotem","mulgore","pools of vision","spirit rise","thunder bluff"},["n"]="Thunder Bluff",["s"]="Thunder Bluff is the magnificent capital city of the tauren, perched atop windswept red-rock mesas rising above the grasslands of Mulgore. Founded by High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof after uniting the nomadic tribes with the aid of the New Horde, it serves as the cultural, spiritual, and mercantile heart of his people. Connected by swaying rope bridges and wooden lifts, the city offers safety from centaur marauders and a peaceful sanctuary for travelers across Kalimdor.",["sec"]={{["b"]="For countless generations, the tauren lived as nomadic wanderers across Kalimdor, locked in a brutal struggle for survival against ravenous centaur tribes. That desperate era ended during the Third War when Cairne Bloodhoof joined forces with Warchief Thrall. Together, the orcs and tauren broke the centaur assaults and secured the lush pastures of Mulgore. To protect their future, Cairne led the allied tribes to the safety of these natural stone bastions, founding Thunder Bluff as the first unified, permanent home the tauren had possessed in centuries.",["t"]="A Permanent Haven"},{["b"]="Spanning several high mesas linked by suspended rope bridges, Thunder Bluff seamlessly blends natural architecture with tauren practicality. The central bluffs host the bustling markets, bank, inn, and towering Wind Rider roost, while three specialized mesas branch out across the abyss. Hunter Rise serves the city's scouts and trackers; Elder Rise hosts lorekeepers and the Cenarion Circle under Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem; and Spirit Rise is devoted to spiritual communion, sheltering shamans, priests, and the mysterious subterranean caverns known as the Pools of Vision.",["t"]="A City in the Clouds"},{["b"]="While Cairne Bloodhoof commands immense respect from almost every tribe, governing a recently settled, proud people is not without friction. Notably, the Grimtotem tribe—led by the shrewd and sharp-tongued Elder Crone Magatha Grimtotem—maintains a brooding presence on Elder Rise. Magatha openly questions Cairne's conciliatory methods and allegiance to the foreign races of the Horde. Though peace endures beneath the chieftain's watchful gaze, ideological rifts murmur among the council fires above the plains.",["t"]="Uneasy Unity"}}}}}
t["zone:undercity"] = {["faq"]={{["a"]="Bajo el palacio de Lordaeron se extendían las antiguas criptas de la realeza humana, junto con el complejo de mazmorras y la vasta red de alcantarillado de la ciudad. Albergaba prisioneros de guerra, incluido Orgrim Martillo Maldito, así como a ciudadanos desfavorecidos que vivían entre las sombras subterráneas. Tras saquear la capital, el príncipe Arthas comenzó a convertir estas catacumbas en una fortaleza de la Plaga antes de que Sylvanas se apoderase de ellas.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="f1e09e",["i"]=1,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="La ciudad superficial de Lordaeron quedó completamente destruida durante la Tercera Guerra, lo que dificultaba su defensa frente a la Cruzada Escarlata, las fuerzas de la Plaga y los supervivientes de la Alianza. Los túneles subterráneos ofrecían una fortificación natural, puntos de estrangulamiento y discreción, permitiendo a los Renegados completar la construcción inicial de la Plaga y prosperar sin ser vistos.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="ebed60",["i"]=2,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="La ciudad está gobernada por Lady Sylvanas Brisaveloz, la Reina Alma en Pena y Reina de los Renegados. Administra su reino desde el Barrio Real, protegida por centinelas reales renegados y su subyugado lugarteniente señor del terror, Varimathras.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="cf0afe",["i"]=3,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Sobre los ascensores principales de la ciudad reposa un monumento de piedra para el difunto rey Terenas Menethil II. No alberga sus restos, ya que su cadáver fue incinerado y sus cenizas esparcidas durante la resurrección de Kel'Thuzad. Valientes supervivientes humanos de la Alianza esculpieron el monumento entre las ruinas para que perdurara el recuerdo del último verdadero rey de Lordaeron.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="8b3ccd",["i"]=4,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="En la derruida sala del trono situada sobre los ascensores de Entrañas, aún persisten ecos espectrales del pasado. Quienes se sitúen en el centro pueden escuchar tenues sonidos fantasmales: las campanas de celebración y los pétalos de rosa del regreso de Arthas, las palabras de advertencia del profeta Medivh y la conversación final entre el rey Terenas y su parricida hijo.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="30d525",["i"]=5,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Los Renegados sellaron una alianza por conveniencia con la Horda del Jefe de Guerra Thrall para sobrevivir, rodeados como están por los hostiles fanáticos escarlata y las legiones de la Plaga. Aunque a las razas vivas de la Horda los vapores tóxicos y los ríos alquímicos les resultan asfixiantes, se concede paso a emisarios y viajeros para fomentar los lazos diplomáticos y militares.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="286722",["i"]=6,["q"]="",["sp"]=0}},["fp"]="9,400,36.527.0,20.525.0,26.518.0,54.383.0,60.340.0,24.216.0,51.333.0,57.328.0,51.322.0",["h"]="13cf60",["n"]="Entrañas",["s"]="Entrañas. En las profundidades de las ruinas devastadas de la Ciudad Capital de Lordaeron se halla Entrañas, el oscuro bastión de los Renegados. Excavada originalmente en antiguas criptas reales y mazmorras por la Plaga, fue tomada por Lady Sylvanas Brisaveloz y transformada en un santuario subterráneo impenetrable. Hoy en día sirve como sede del poder de los Renegados y vital bastión de la Horda en el norte de los Reinos del Este. Mucho antes de que la plaga asolara Lordaeron, las catacumbas, mazmorras y cloacas situadas bajo el palacio se conocían colectivamente como Entrañas. Albergaban las tumbas de la nobleza, las prisiones más profundas del reino —donde el antiguo Jefe de Guerra Orgrim Martillo Maldito estuvo recluido antes de su fuga— y a los habitantes más pobres del territorio. Cuando Arthas Menethil asesinó al rey Terenas y arrasó la capital en el año 20 d. P., el príncipe ordenó a sus sirvientes no-muertos excavar y ampliar los pasadizos subterráneos para convertirlos en una sede fortificada del dominio de la Plaga.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,0}},["text"]={["ang"]={["faction:Horde"]="As the Horde's primary foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms, the Undercity secures zeppelin links directly back to Durotar and Grom'gol Base Camp.",["race:Human"]="Beneath the soil of your fallen ancestral kingdom lies a grim perversion of Lordaeron's royal crypts, occupied by the walking corpses of your former brethren.",["race:Orc"]="Deep within these very dungeons, the legendary Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer was once imprisoned by the Alliance of Lordaeron before making his historic escape.",["race:Undead"]="The Undercity is your sovereign sanctuary and homeland, carved out by your own hands after breaking the Lich King's iron grip on your mind."},["faq"]={{["a"]="Beneath the palace of Lordaeron lay the ancient crypts of human royalty, along with the city's dungeon complex and vast sewer network. It housed prisoners of war, including Orgrim Doomhammer, as well as destitute citizens living in the subterranean shadows. After sacking the capital, Prince Arthas began turning these catacombs into a Scourge fortress before Sylvanas captured them.",["al"]={"undercity origins","what was undercity originally","lordaeron sewers history"},["q"]="What was the Undercity before the undead took it over?"},{["a"]="The surface city of Lordaeron was utterly ruined during the Third War, making it difficult to defend against the Scarlet Crusade, Scourge forces, and Alliance survivors. The subterranean tunnels offered natural fortification, choke points, and secrecy, allowing the Forsaken to complete the Scourge's initial construction and thrive unseen.",["al"]={"why live underground","why not rebuild lordaeron","why undercity beneath ruins"},["q"]="Why is the city built underground instead of on the surface?"},{["a"]="The city is ruled by Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the Banshee Queen and Queen of the Forsaken. She governs from the Royal Quarter, guarded by Forsaken royal sentries and her subjugated dreadlord lieutenant, Varimathras.",["al"]={"who is leader of undercity","undercity ruler","who runs undercity"},["q"]="Who rules the Undercity?"},{["a"]="Above the city's main lifts sits a stone memorial for the slain King Terenas Menethil II. It does not contain his body, as his corpse was cremated and his ashes discarded during Kel'Thuzad's resurrection. Surviving Alliance humans bravely carved the monument amidst the ruins so the memory of Lordaeron's last true king would endure.",["al"]={"king terenas tomb","lordaeron throne room grave","who built the tomb above undercity"},["q"]="What is King Terenas's memorial in the ruins above?"},{["a"]="In the ruined throne room above the Undercity lifts, spectral echoes of the past still linger. Visitors standing in the center can hear faint phantom sounds: the celebratory bells and rose petals of Arthas's return, the warning words of the prophet Medivh, and the final conversation between King Terenas and his patricidal son.",["al"]={"ghost sounds throne room","throne room whispering","voices in lordaeron throne room"},["q"]="What are the strange noises in the Lordaeron throne room?"},{["a"]="The Forsaken formed an alliance of convenience with Warchief Thrall's Horde for survival, surrounded as they are by hostile Scarlet zealots and Scourge legions. While living Horde races find the toxic fumes and alchemical rivers stifling, emissaries and travelers are allowed access to foster diplomatic and military ties.",["al"]={"why are orcs in undercity","horde in undercity","why is undercity part of horde"},["q"]="Why do the Forsaken allow other Horde members here?"}},["kw"]={"apothecarium","arthas","forsaken","lordaeron","scourge","sylvanas","terenas","tirisfal","undercity","varimathras"},["n"]="Undercity",["s"]="Deep beneath the shattered ruins of Lordaeron's Capital City lies the Undercity, the dark stronghold of the Forsaken. Originally carved out of ancient royal crypts and dungeons by the Scourge, it was seized by Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and remade into an impenetrable subterranean sanctuary. Today it serves as the seat of Forsaken power and the Horde's vital bastion in the northern Eastern Kingdoms.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Long before the plague swept Lordaeron, the catacombs, dungeons, and sewers beneath the palace were collectively known as the Undercity. It held the tombs of nobility, the kingdom's deepest prisons—where the former Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer was once held before his escape—and the poorest denizens of the realm. When Arthas Menethil murdered King Terenas and laid waste to the capital in 20 ADP, the prince ordered his undead servants to excavate and expand the subterranean passages into a fortified seat of Scourge rule.",["t"]="From Royal Crypts to Scourge Redoubt"},{["b"]="The Scourge never saw their dark capital completed. When the Lich King's powers waned following the battle of Mount Hyjal, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner broke free from the hive mind, taking thousands of undead with her. She lured Arthas into an ambush and drove him back to Northrend. After a vicious three-way civil war against Scourge remnants, dreadlords, and human resistance, Sylvanas claimed the half-finished catacombs. Naming her people the Forsaken, she oversaw years of labor completing the sprawling subterranean city.",["t"]="Rise of the Forsaken"},{["b"]="The Undercity is laid out in concentric rings divided into functional quarters. At its heart lies the Trade Quarter, connected by great lifts to the palace ruins above. Surrounding it are the War Quarter, Rogues' Quarter, Magic Quarter, and the toxic Apothecarium, where the Royal Apothecary Society brews virulent plagues in green slime-filled canals. Deeper still lies the Royal Quarter, where the Banshee Queen holds court alongside her dreadlord commander, Varimathras, guarded by legions of towering abominations.",["t"]="Architecture of the Damned"}}}}}
t["zone:ungoro"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="14,289,13.564.0,18.559.0,16.579.0,35.395.0,45.390.0,56.325.0,51.316.0,30.275.0,52.274.0",["h"]="465c6d",["n"]="Cráter de Un'Goro",["s"]="Cráter de Un'Goro. El Cráter de Un'Goro es una jungla primordial y hundida, encajada entre los áridos páramos de Tanaris y Silithus. Dominada por dinosaurios errantes, elementales vivientes y un volcán central, esta exuberante caldera está repleta de antiguas reliquias de los titanes y cristales de poder cuyos orígenes siguen siendo un profundo misterio. Emparedado entre extensiones desérticas, el Cráter de Un'Goro desafía la geografía del sur de Kalimdor. Los antiguos registros de los elfos de la noche que detallan la Guerra del Mar de Dunas señalan que los insectoides qiraji fueron repelidos en los bordes del cráter, incapaces de penetrar en lo que en su lengua llamaban las «Tierras de los Dioses». Los eruditos teorizan que la caldera sirvió como campo de pruebas o antigua morada para los titanes durante la creación de Kalimdor. A lo largo de las crestas se alzan pilones de piedra que vibran con energía, mientras formaciones de cristales azules, rojos, verdes y amarillos brotan a través de la tierra primigenia.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,0}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Druid"]="Cenarion Circle druids study Un'Goro's isolated ecosystem and harvest its rich soil to propagate Morrowgrain, seeking to understand the pure, untamed life force sustaining the caldera.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance naturalists rally around Williden Marshal and J.D. Collie at Marshal's Refuge, racing to decipher the Titan pylons while safeguarding Darnassus researchers gathering soil samples.",["faction:Horde"]="Horde adventurers often arrive via Tanaris to test their prowess against legendary devilsaurs and assist the herbalists of Thunder Bluff in cultivating Morrowgrain from Un'Goro's primeval dirt.",["profession:Herbalism"]="The rich moisture of the basin sustains rare flora, serving as one of the few places in Kalimdor where Blindweed, Dreamfoil, and Golden Sansam flourish together alongside Firebloom on the volcano's slopes.",["profession:Mining"]="The sheer crater walls and the crags of Fire Plume Ridge are dense with Mithril Deposits and Rich Thorium Veins, making the zone a premier destination for high-end ore."},["faq"]={{["a"]="Travelers can enter via natural ramps leading down the crater walls: one accessible from the southwestern corner of Tanaris near Thistleshrub Valley, and another leading in from northeastern Silithus. While reckless adventurers sometimes leap down the cliffs, the fall is fatal without magical assistance. Marshal's Refuge in the north provides flight connections to Gadgetzan and Cenarion Hold.",["al"]={"how to enter ungoro","ungoro entrance","how to reach ungoro crater"},["q"]="How do you get into Un'Goro Crater?"},{["a"]="Scattered across the jungle floor are red, blue, green, and yellow Power Crystals. With guidance from the gnome researcher J.D. Collie at Marshal's Refuge, explorers can decipher instructions for using these crystals at the Northern, Eastern, and Western Pylons. Combining specific colors at the appropriate pylons harnesses latent Titan energies to fashion defensive or restorative charms.",["al"]={"ungoro crystals","red blue green yellow crystals ungoro","power pylons"},["q"]="What are the colored power crystals used for?"},{["a"]="Scholars believe Un'Goro's unnatural vibrancy is artificial, stemming from the Titans who shaped the world. Titanic conduits, warm volcanic geothermal activity from Fire Plume Ridge, and ancient ley energy nourish an isolated, hyper-accelerated ecosystem that resists the arid conditions of neighbouring Tanaris and Silithus.",["al"]={"why is ungoro a jungle","ungoro desert connection","how does ungoro have plants"},["q"]="Why is Un'Goro lush despite being surrounded by deserts?"},{["a"]="According to tablets translated from the War of the Shifting Sands, the insect swarms halted their advance at the crater's threshold. Geologist Larksbane, who translated the account, ties the name to theories that the Titans once dwelt in the crater, and wonders whether Aman'Thul himself watched over the God Lands.",["al"]={"god lands meaning","qiraji ungoro","why couldn't silithid take ungoro"},["q"]="Why did the Qiraji call this place the 'God Lands'?"},{["a"]="Piles of rich, potent dirt rest across the crater basin. When harvested, this soil can be paired with Tharlendris seeds in an Evergreen Pouch to cultivate Morrowgrain, a mystical herb intensely sought after by researchers in Thunder Bluff, Darnassus, and the Cenarion Circle.",["al"]={"ungoro soil sample","what does ungoro soil do","morrowgrain soil"},["q"]="What is Un'Goro Soil used for?"},{["a"]="There are no instances, dungeons, or indigenous humanoid societies in Un'Goro Crater. The only lasting settlement is Marshal's Refuge, a small expedition outpost on the northern rim. Beyond isolated hermit hunters or lost explorers, the rest of the caldera is entirely wild.",["al"]={"ungoro dungeons","who lives in ungoro","humanoids in ungoro"},["q"]="Are there any humanoid settlements or dungeons here?"}},["kw"]={"devilsaur","fire plume ridge","god lands","lakkari","marshal's refuge","power crystals","torwa pathfinder","ungoro","ungoro crater"},["n"]="Un'Goro Crater",["s"]="Un'Goro Crater is a sunken, primordial jungle tucked between the arid wastes of Tanaris and Silithus. Dominated by roaming dinosaurs, living elementals, and a central volcano, this lush caldera is filled with ancient Titan relics and power crystals whose origins remain a profound mystery.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Sandwiched between desert expanses, Un'Goro Crater defies the geography of southern Kalimdor. Ancient night elf records detailing the War of the Shifting Sands note that the insectoid Qiraji were turned back at the crater's edges, unable to penetrate what their tongue named the 'God Lands.' Scholars theorize the caldera served as a testing ground or ancient dwelling for the Titans during the shaping of Kalimdor. Stone pylons rise along the ridges, humming with energy, while crystal formations of blue, red, green, and yellow sprout through the primeval earth.",["t"]="The God Lands"},{["b"]="Un'Goro contains no cities, towns, or native humanoid societies. Instead, life thrives in an untamed, primeval state. Apex devilsaurs stalk the flats of Terror Run, predatory bloodpetals lash out in the marshlands, and oozes creep across the tar pits of Lakkari. At the heart of the crater looms Fire Plume Ridge, an active volcanic peak teeming with hostile fire elementals led by the entity Blazerunner. For explorers and skinners, the basin offers unmatched natural wealth—including the coveted hides of devilsaurs—provided one survives the food chain.",["t"]="A Savage Ecosystem"},{["b"]="Civilized presence in the crater is limited almost entirely to Marshal's Refuge, an elevated camp tucked into the northern cliffs. Led by Williden Marshal, an expedition of scientists, naturalists, and fortune seekers attempted to catalogue the basin's treasures only to be battered by the monstrous wildlife and driven to high ground. Today, the refuge serves as an embattled staging ground for intrepid travelers studying the crater's mysterious power crystals, mapping volcanic activity, and keeping a watchful eye on encroaching silithid swarms breaching the southern ridges.",["t"]="Marshal's Refuge"}}}}}
t["zone:westernplaguelands"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="19,377,28.525.0,18.542.0,16.525.0,41.284.0,37.251.0,56.229.0,58.284.0,44.220.0,51.244.0",["h"]="c61f26",["n"]="Tierras de la Peste del Oeste",["s"]="Tierras de la Peste del Oeste. Antaño el fértil granero agrícola del norte de Lordaeron, las Tierras de la Peste del Oeste se asfixian ahora bajo una niebla tóxica y legiones de no-muertos. Aunque las calderas de Kel'Thuzad arruinaron las granjas y aldeas de la región, la tierra aún se aferra tenazmente a la vida. El Alba Argenta, la Alianza, los Renegados y la Cruzada Escarlata libran desesperadas campañas a lo largo de sus campos marchitos. Antes de la Tercera Guerra, esta tierra era un verde pasillo de granjas que se extendía al este desde los Claros de Tirisfal hacia el Lago Darrowmere. Vega del Amparo gobernaba la administración norteña desde el Fuerte de Mardenholde, mientras que la extensa villa de Andorhal coordinaba el almacenamiento y la exportación de grano a lo largo del reino. Ese crucial papel agrícola convirtió a la región en el objetivo primordial de Kel'Thuzad. Al introducir la plaga de la no-muerte directamente en los cargamentos de grano de Andorhal, el Culto de los Malditos convirtió el sustento de Lordaeron en su perdición.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,0}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Paladin"]="The desecrated fields of Lordaeron are a somber trial for holy warriors, offering pilgrimage to Uther's Tomb as well as righteous war against the horrors of Scholomance and Hearthglen's corrupted zealots.",["faction:Alliance"]="Chillwind Camp serves as the forward Alliance garrison, where Commander Ashlam Valorfist directs campaigns to disrupt the plague cauldrons and avenge the fallen cities of Lordaeron.",["faction:Horde"]="The Bulwark marks the frontier of Forsaken sovereignty; from here, the Horde pushes eastward to neutralize Scourge commanders and contest Scarlet zealots along their vulnerable flank.",["profession:Herbalism"]="The lingering taint makes the region one of the few places where rare, dangerous flora like Plaguebloom and Arthas' Tears can take root among blighted pine groves."},["faq"]={{["a"]="Andorhal was the agricultural shipping hub of northern Lordaeron. Kel'Thuzad intentionally targeted the regional supply, infecting grain silos and installing massive plague cauldrons across four major farmsteads to rapidly distribute undeath through food shipments across the kingdom.",["al"]={"why is western plaguelands plagued","what happened to andorhal","why cauldrons here"},["q"]="Why did the Plague hit this area so hard?"},{["a"]="Hearthglen and its fortress, Mardenholde Keep, are ruled by the Scarlet Crusade. Though originally a regional administrative hub for Lordaeron, the zealots now execute any outsiders who approach their borders, viewing all non-crusaders with suspicion.",["al"]={"hearthglen faction","who lives in mardenholde keep","is hearthglen friendly"},["q"]="Who controls Hearthglen in the north?"},{["a"]="The Alliance maintains Chillwind Camp near the southern border with the Alterac Mountains, providing a flight master and Argent Dawn allies. The Horde operates from the Bulwark on the western border entering from Tirisfal Glades.",["al"]={"where are the flight paths","horde camp wpl","alliance camp wpl"},["q"]="Where can Alliance and Horde players find safe outposts?"},{["a"]="Erected by the Cult of the Damned at Felstone Field, Dalson's Tears, the Writhing Haunt, and Gahrron's Withering, these cauldrons pump constant plague vapors into the air and soil. Argent Dawn agents recruit adventurers to slay the Cauldron Lords and counter-engineer the toxic brews.",["al"]={"what are the cauldrons","cauldron purpose","felstone cauldron"},["q"]="What is the purpose of the plague cauldrons in the fields?"},{["a"]="Scholomance is a subterranean academy of necromancy run by the Scourge and the Cult of the Damned. It is hidden beneath the ruins of Caer Darrow, an island fortress situated in the southeastern waters of Darrowmere Lake.",["al"]={"scholomance location","where is scholomance","caer darrow dungeon"},["q"]="What is Scholomance and where is it located?"},{["a"]="Uther's Tomb, located on Sorrow Hill near the southern edge of the zone, remains entirely holy and untouched by blight. It stands as a consecrated memorial to the fallen High General Uther the Lightbringer and the lost Order of the Silver Hand.",["al"]={"holy ground in wpl","is uther tomb clean","uther's tomb safe"},["q"]="Is any part of the Western Plaguelands uncorrupted?"}},["kw"]={"andorhal","bulwark","caer darrow","cauldrons","chillwind camp","hearthglen","mardenholde keep","scholomance","uther's tomb","western plaguelands"},["n"]="Western Plaguelands",["s"]="Once the fertile agricultural breadbasket of northern Lordaeron, the Western Plaguelands now choke beneath a toxic mist and legions of undead. While Kel'Thuzad's cauldrons ruined the region's farms and towns, the soil still clings tenaciously to life. The Argent Dawn, the Alliance, the Forsaken, and the Scarlet Crusade all wage desperate campaigns across its blighted fields.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Before the Third War, this land was a verdant corridor of farms stretching east from Tirisfal Glades toward Darrowmere Lake. Hearthglen governed the northern administration from Mardenholde Keep, while the sprawling town of Andorhal orchestrated the storage and export of grain across the kingdom. That crucial agricultural role made the region Kel'Thuzad's primary target. By introducing the Plague of Undeath directly into the grain shipments at Andorhal, the Cult of the Damned turned Lordaeron's sustenance into its doom.",["t"]="The Breadbasket of Lordaeron"},{["b"]="To permanently poison the soil, the Scourge raised four massive plague cauldrons across the largest agricultural estates: Felstone Field, Dalson's Tears, the Writhing Haunt, and Gahrron's Withering. Today, monstrous Cauldron Lords maintain these noxious vats, pumping venomous fog into the atmosphere. The surrounding trees twist with swollen, tumorous growths, and blighted wildlife rampages through the brush. Beneath Caer Darrow on Darrowmere Lake, Darkmaster Gandling trains necromancers within Scholomance, compounding the undead threat.",["t"]="A Land Under Siege"},{["b"]="Despite the omnipresence of the Scourge, several factions maintain embattled footholds. The Scarlet Crusade controls the fortress of Hearthglen in the north, purging anything they deem tainted with rabid zeal. To the south, Alliance forces stage from Chillwind Camp, while the Forsaken defend their borders at the Bulwark. Mediating and directing the fight against the undead cauldrons is the neutral Argent Dawn, which aids champions of both factions and guards Uther's Tomb—the last truly sanctified ground in the region.",["t"]="Divided Factions"}}}}}
t["zone:westfall"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="8,337,18.502.0,27.479.0,20.427.0,24.379.1,44.289.0,52.262.0,29.269.0,45.267.0,32.226.1,51.200.0",["h"]="10c904",["n"]="Páramos de Poniente",["s"]="Páramos de Poniente. En su día el fértil granero del Reino de Ventormenta, Páramos de Poniente se ha desmoronado hasta convertirse en un territorio azotado por el viento y abandonado, infestado de forajidos. Privados del apoyo oficial de la corona y de guarniciones militares, los colonos supervivientes se aferran a sus granjas mientras la Hermandad Defias afianza su dominio desde el pueblo minero abandonado de Arroyo de la Luna. Durante generaciones, Páramos de Poniente alimentó al Reino de Ventormenta. Sus ondulantes pastos y trigales prosperaron hasta que las secuelas de la Segunda Guerra trajeron el agotamiento financiero y la negligencia política. Cuando el reino volcó sus recursos en otros lugares y no logró mantener sus guarniciones, el territorio quedó en barbecho. Hoy en día, los pastos yermos, las aullantes ráfagas costeras, los gólems de la cosecha descontrolados y los saqueos de los gnolls Zarparrío convierten la supervivencia diaria en una dura prueba constante para los pocos y obstinados habitantes que se niegan a abandonar sus tierras.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,1,0},["sec"]={0,0,0,1}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Rogue"]="The shadow war across Westfall pits SI:7 spies directly against the street-smart scouts, thieves, and assassins of the Defias Brotherhood.",["faction:Alliance"]="Without the People's Militia holding Sentinel Hill, the Defias Brotherhood would control the southern approaches straight to Elwynn Forest.",["profession:Cooking"]="The beleaguered homesteaders at Saldean's Farm make do with tough local beasts, teaching travelers to brew the hearty staple known as Westfall Stew.",["race:Human"]="Westfall is a painful reflection of Stormwind's political decay; the farmers starving in the dirt were abandoned by the very nobility they toiled to feed."},["faq"]={{["a"]="Stormwind withdrew its military garrisons when upkeep became too expensive, leaving the region unprotected. Soon after, the Defias Brotherhood seized control of the countryside, driving off homesteaders, burning cropland, and reprogramming mechanical harvest watchers into lethal sentries.",["al"]={"abandoned farms","why is westfall dead","why fallow"},["q"]="Why are so many farms abandoned in Westfall?"},{["a"]="The kingdom's treasury is strained, and the House of Nobles considers threats in other lands to be higher priorities. Furthermore, court manipulation has quietly starved Westfall of attention and supplies, forcing local residents to fend entirely for themselves.",["al"]={"why no stormwind army","where are the guards","stormwind aid"},["q"]="Why does Stormwind not send troops to help Westfall?"},{["a"]="The People's Militia is a volunteer defense outfit organized by Gryan Stoutmantle at Sentinel Hill. Because the crown refused to deploy soldiers to Westfall, the remaining farmers and settlers banded together to fight off the Defias Brotherhood and the Riverpaw gnolls.",["al"]={"peoples militia","sentinel hill militia","gryan stoutmantle army"},["q"]="What is the People's Militia?"},{["a"]="Edwin VanCleef, the former guildmaster of the Stonemasons Guild who oversaw the rebuilding of Stormwind City. After a bitter dispute over payment resulted in riots and the death of Queen Tiffin, VanCleef led his workers into Westfall and turned to armed insurrection.",["al"]={"defias leader","who leads defias","vancleef"},["q"]="Who leads the Defias Brotherhood in Westfall?"},{["a"]="Underneath an unassuming barn in Moonbrook lies the entrance to the Deadmines, an extensive abandoned gold mine that the Defias Brotherhood converted into an underground fortress and shipwright for their war against Stormwind.",["al"]={"moonbrook secret","what is in deadmines","moonbrook mine"},["q"]="What is hidden inside Moonbrook?"},{["a"]="Originally built to tend the crops and scare away pests, these mechanical harvest watchers were captured and retrofitted by Defias-aligned goblin tinkerers to patrol fields and murder any trespassers.",["al"]={"harvest golems attacking","harvest watchers","evil scarecrows"},["q"]="Why are the mechanical scarecrows attacking people?"}},["kw"]={"deadmines","defias","harvest golem","militia","moonbrook","saldean","sentinel hill","stoutmantle","vancleef","westfall"},["n"]="Westfall",["s"]="Once the fertile breadbasket of the Kingdom of Stormwind, Westfall has collapsed into a windswept, neglected territory overrun by outlaws. Deprived of official crown support and military garrisons, its surviving homesteaders cling to their farms while the Defias Brotherhood tightens its grip from the abandoned mining town of Moonbrook.",["sec"]={{["b"]="For generations, Westfall fed the Kingdom of Stormwind. Its rolling pastures and wheat fields flourished until the aftermath of the Second War brought financial exhaustion and political neglect. When the kingdom poured its resources elsewhere and failed to maintain its garrisons, the territory grew fallow. Today, barren pastures, howling coastal gusts, rogue harvest golems, and raiding Riverpaw gnolls make daily survival a constant trial for the stubborn few who refuse to abandon their homesteads.",["t"]="A Land Left Behind"},{["b"]="Westfall's collapse accelerated when the Stonemasons Guild, denied payment for rebuilding Stormwind City, turned to open revolt. Outlawed and exiled under Edwin VanCleef, the stonemasons reconstituted themselves as the Defias Brotherhood. They claimed Westfall as their stronghold, burning stubborn farmers' fields, salting soil, and seizing abandoned mines. Operating out of the ghost town of Moonbrook, the Brotherhood now rules the majority of the countryside in all but name.",["t"]="The Rise of the Brotherhood"},{["b"]="With the House of Nobles ignoring Westfall's plight, local inhabitants took up their own defense. Based at the fortified post of Sentinel Hill, Gryan Stoutmantle organized the People's Militia—a volunteer force attempting to hold the line against gnolls and bandits. Lacking formal soldiers and supplies from Stormwind, the Militia relies on wandering adventurers to push back the Defias tide and protect the remaining farms.",["t"]="The People's Militia"},{["b"]="The true scale of the Defias threat lies concealed beneath the surface. Deep within the abandoned gold shafts of the Deadmines beneath Moonbrook, Edwin VanCleef has assembled an army of cutthroats, goblins, and renegades. Rather than simple banditry, the Brotherhood is constructing a massive ironclad juggernaut designed to sail up the coast and bombard Stormwind City directly.",["t"]="The Deadmines Conspiracy"}}}}}
t["zone:winterspring"] = {["faq"]={},["fp"]="12,467,26.561.0,23.475.0,26.540.0,31.471.0,29.342.0,30.316.0,45.381.0,43.326.0,28.272.0,26.260.0,39.291.0",["h"]="f3b4af",["n"]="Cuna del Invierno",["s"]="Cuna del Invierno. Cuna del Invierno es un vasto valle cubierto de nieve ubicado en lo alto de las laderas nororientales del Monte Hyjal, justo por encima de los bosques encantados de Claro de la Luna y Frondavil. Conocida por su frío cortante, antiguas ruinas Altonato y formidables depredadores errantes, la región se articula en torno al puesto neutral goblin de Vista Eterna. Sigue siendo una tierra repleta de feroces peligros naturales que acoge a supervivientes del Vuelo Azul mientras combate la corrupción demoníaca a lo largo de sus cumbres meridionales. Encaramada a miles de metros sobre la naturaleza que la rodea, Cuna del Invierno descansa bajo un aire alpino glacial e implacables tormentas de nieve. Las enormes raíces expuestas del Árbol del Mundo, Nordrassil, atraviesan las acumulaciones de nieve como altísimos arcos de madera, deformadas y retorcidas durante las trascendentales batallas de la Tercera Guerra. Mientras que gran parte del norte de Kalimdor está dominada por densas arboledas y climas cálidos, Cuna del Invierno es una tierra apacible de bosques alpinos, peñascos rocosos y lagos helados, hogar de temibles sables de hielo, quimeras, yetis y osos invernales de espeso pelaje.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,0,0}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Druid"]="Located high above Moonglade, Winterspring holds sacred significance as the place where Malfurion Stormrage awakened the Druids of the Talon from their ancient sleep during the Third War.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance heroes frequently visit Rivern Frostwind at Frostsaber Rock, working tirelessly in the extreme cold to earn the loyalty of the prestigious Winterspring Frostsaber mounts.",["profession:Herbalism"]="Winterspring is the only known habitat for Icecap, an indispensable alpine herb heavily sought after for advanced alchemical concoctions, along with scattered Mountain Silversage and rare Black Lotus blooms.",["profession:Mining"]="The icy crags and cavern walls across the valley are rich with valuable minerals, frequently yielding Small and Rich Thorium Veins alongside rare Truesilver deposits.",["race:NightElf"]="Winterspring is intimately tied to elven history, featuring the barrow dens where the Druids of the Talon rested for millennia, the tragic ruins of Kel'Theril, and exposed roots of Nordrassil damaged during the Battle of Mount Hyjal."},["faq"]={{["a"]="Travelers must journey through Timbermaw Hold, a subterranean network of tunnels located in northeastern Felwood. Navigating the caves peacefully requires earning the trust of the native Timbermaw furbolgs, who will attack any trespassers they deem hostile. Once through the eastern exit, the snowy slopes of Winterspring open up immediately.",["al"]={"getting to winterspring","how to enter winterspring","winterspring entrance","timbermaw tunnel winterspring"},["q"]="How do I get to Winterspring?"},{["a"]="Everlook is managed by the Steamwheedle Cartel, the goblin trading conglomerate. Because of the goblins' strict profit motive and neutrality, both Horde and Alliance travelers can access its flight masters, repair vendors, banking vaults, and trade goods, provided visitors do not break the peace within town limits.",["al"]={"who controls everlook","everlook faction","is everlook neutral"},["q"]="Who runs the town of Everlook?"},{["a"]="They are the spirits of Highborne night elves who once inhabited the ancient city of Kel'Theril ten thousand years ago. When their sorcerers tampered with the stolen Crystal of Zin-Malor, its shattering unleashed a catastrophic curse. Subsequent expeditions of high elves centuries later were similarly driven to madness, doomed to haunt the frozen ruins alongside their ancestors.",["al"]={"lake kel'theril ghosts","ruins of kel'theril spirits","who is at kel'theril"},["q"]="What are the ghosts haunting Lake Kel'Theril?"},{["a"]="The Winterfall furbolgs have succumbed to a spreading, unnatural corruption that has driven their clan into maddened hostility. While the nearby Timbermaw hold tightly to their sanity, the Winterfall have fallen under dark influences, prompting researchers like Donova Snowden to study their taint and clear their encampments.",["al"]={"why are winterfall hostile","winterfall tribe corruption","donova snowden furbolgs"},["q"]="Why are the Winterfall furbolgs aggressive?"},{["a"]="Mazthoril is the primary roost and sanctuary of the blue dragonflight on Kalimdor. Deep within its cavernous spires, wyrmkin, elite whelps, and ancient drakes guard powerful draconic relics and arcane secrets under the watchful eye of Haleh, consort to the Aspect Malygos.",["al"]={"mazthoril dragons","what is mazthoril","blue dragons in winterspring"},["q"]="What lives inside Mazthoril?"},{["a"]="Darkwhisper Gorge is a treacherous canyon in the southernmost reaches of the valley, occupied by powerful elite demons leftover from the Third War. It serves as a heavily guarded perimeter along the treacherous trails leading toward the heights of Mount Hyjal.",["al"]={"darkwhisper gorge demons","what is in darkwhisper gorge","hyjal gate winterspring"},["q"]="What is Darkwhisper Gorge?"},{["a"]="At Frostsaber Rock in the far northern reaches of the valley, the night elf Rivern Frostwind tends to the native white cats. Adventurers who assist him with securing provisions and safeguarding the pride may prove themselves worthy of learning the secrets to rearing and riding a Frostsaber.",["al"]={"frostsaber mount","rivern frostwind frostsaber","how to get frostsaber"},["q"]="Can you train or tame frostsabers here?"}},["kw"]={"blue dragons","darkwhisper gorge","donova snowden","everlook","frostsaber","icecap","kel'theril","mazthoril","timbermaw","winterspring"},["n"]="Winterspring",["s"]="Winterspring is a vast, snow-covered valley nestled high along the northeastern slopes of Mount Hyjal, directly above the enchanted forests of Moonglade and Felwood. Known for its bitter cold, ancient Highborne ruins, and wandering apex predators, the region is anchored by the neutral goblin outpost of Everlook. It remains a land of fierce natural perils, sheltering remnants of the blue dragonflight while confronting demonic corruption along its southern heights.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Perched thousands of feet above the surrounding wilderness, Winterspring rests beneath crisp mountain air and relentless snowstorms. Enormous exposed roots of the World Tree, Nordrassil, cut through the snowdrifts like towering wooden arches, warped and twisted during the climactic struggles of the Third War. While much of northern Kalimdor is dominated by dense woodlands and warm climates, Winterspring is a quiet land of medium alpine forests, rocky bluffs, and frozen lakes, home to formidable frostsabers, chimaeras, yetis, and thick-furred winter bears.",["t"]="A Realm of Perpetual Frost"},{["b"]="Reaching this high plateau requires traversing the ancient carved passages of Timbermaw Hold, guarded by uncorrupted furbolgs who closely scrutinize any travelers passing from Felwood or Moonglade. At the valley's center sits Everlook, a thriving trading town operated by the Steamwheedle Cartel. Goblins welcome both Alliance and Horde adventurers with open registers, acting as an indispensable haven of engineering, trade, and warm fires against the biting mountain frost.",["t"]="Crossroads and Outposts"},{["b"]="Centuries before modern settlements arose, ancient elves built the lakeside city of Kel'Theril. After reckless Highborne sorcerers shattered the cursed Crystal of Zin-Malor, madness claimed them, leaving restless phantoms to wander the ruined pillars and frozen waters to this day. Further south rise the cavernous heights of Mazthoril, the traditional Kalimdor stronghold of the blue dragonflight. Here, the brood of the Spell-Weaver Malygos, overseen by the matron protector Haleh, watches over arcane currents and contests local threats.",["t"]="Old Ghosts and Blue Drakes"},{["b"]="The serenity of Winterspring is sharply broken along its southernmost border at Darkwhisper Gorge. There, elite demonic forces, remnant legions from the Third War, crawl along jagged canyons blocking the high approaches toward the summit of Mount Hyjal. Meanwhile, the native Winterfall furbolgs have succumbed to foul corruptions, prompting independent researchers like Donova Snowden to investigate the encroaching taint before the entire valley's wildlife is consumed.",["t"]="The Looming Shadow of the Gorge"}}}}}
t["zone:zephras"] = {["faq"]={{["a"]="La Isla de Zephras está situada en Muro Celeste, el Plano Elemental del aire, en lugar de sobre la superficie de Azeroth. Flota entre las nubes perpetuas y las corrientes de viento de este dominio etéreo.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="63fcac",["i"]=1,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="La isla dependía de la presencia de elementales de aire nativos que antaño coexistían con los cielonatos. Hace varios siglos, estos elementales desaparecieron, dejando que los cimientos mágicos que anclan y preservan la tierra flotante se debiliten y decaigan lentamente con el tiempo.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="97c336",["i"]=2,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Son islas flotantes vecinas en Muro Celeste también pobladas por los cielonatos. La Isla de Zephras perdió la comunicación con ambas cuando los elementales desaparecieron, y los refugiados que huyen informan ahora de que tanto Anvilas como Siroccas se están derrumbando hacia el olvido.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="d234f8",["i"]=3,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Los cielonatos operan muelles celestes en el asentamiento de Valanaar. Los barcos voladores zarpan desde estos muelles a través de la frontera planar hacia Azeroth, aterrizando cerca de Dalaran en las Montañas de Alterac y en la Meseta de Miracielos en Mulgore.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="84b808",["i"]=4,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Los cielonatos son los habitantes que tienen su hogar en la Isla de Zephras y sus reinos celestes hermanos. Tras haber morado en Muro Celeste durante generaciones, ahora afrontan la lenta destrucción de su patria flotante a medida que su magia vinculante se desvanece.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="eb7702",["i"]=5,["q"]="",["sp"]=0},{["a"]="Aparte de las aldeas pacíficas y los refugiados recién llegados, la isla se ve asolada por bandidos locales, garranocturnas cavernícolas y bestias que habitan zonas como la Guarida de Chilladoras y la Caverna Garranoche.",["answerOnly"]=true,["h"]="1f8ee4",["i"]=6,["q"]="",["sp"]=0}},["fp"]="12,364,13.457.0,16.440.0,20.405.0,30.189.0,34.257.0,30.248.0,54.216.0,21.226.0,47.194.0",["h"]="f9332b",["n"]="Isla de Zephras",["s"]="Isla de Zephras. La Isla de Zephras es una isla flotante suspendida en Muro Celeste, el Plano Elemental del aire. Sirve como hogar ancestral de inicio de los cielonatos, cuya sociedad se enfrenta ahora a una crisis existencial a medida que la magia que sustenta la isla se erosiona sin cesar. Con el contacto cortado con los reinos celestes vecinos, sus habitantes luchan contra el colapso elemental y la llegada de refugiados. La Isla de Zephras es una masa de tierra aislada a la deriva en los vientos interminables de Muro Celeste. Durante generaciones, los cielonatos vivieron aquí junto a los elementales de aire nativos que ayudaban a preservar las corrientes mágicas que mantenían la isla a flote. Sin embargo, hace varios siglos, esos custodios elementales desaparecieron por completo. Privados de su presencia y tutela, los encantamientos latentes que sostienen la tierra han comenzado a fallar lentamente, provocando que Zephras se fracture y desestabilice poco a poco.",["sec"]={},["source"]={["sp"]={["faq"]={0,0,0,0,0,0},["sec"]={0,0,0}},["text"]={["ang"]={["class:Shaman"]="Existing directly within the Elemental Plane of air, Zephras Isle showcases what happens to elemental lands when the spirits that balance them suddenly disappear.",["race:Skyborne"]="Zephras Isle is your ancestral home and starting ground, though its lingering beauty masks an urgent crisis of collapsing terrain and fading elemental power."},["faq"]={{["a"]="Zephras Isle is situated inside Skywall, the Elemental Plane of air, rather than upon the surface of Azeroth. It floats among the perpetual clouds and wind currents of this ethereal domain.",["al"]={"zephras isle location","where is zephras","what plane is zephras on"},["q"]="Where is Zephras Isle located?"},{["a"]="The island relied on the presence of native air elementals who once coexisted with the Skyborne. Several centuries ago, these elementals vanished, leaving the magical foundations that anchor and preserve the flying land to slowly weaken and decay over time.",["al"]={"why is zephras collapsing","zephras isle destruction","dying island"},["q"]="Why is Zephras Isle falling apart?"},{["a"]="They are neighboring floating islands in Skywall also populated by the Skyborne. Zephras Isle lost communication with both when the elementals disappeared, and fleeing refugees now report that both Anvilas and Siroccas are collapsing into oblivion.",["al"]={"anvilas","siroccas","other skyborne islands"},["q"]="What are Anvilas and Siroccas?"},{["a"]="The Skyborne operate skydocks at the settlement of Valanaar. Airships launch from these docks across the planar boundary to Azeroth, landing near Dalaran in the Alterac Mountains and at Skywatcher Plateau in Mulgore.",["al"]={"how to leave zephras","zephras airships","travel to azeroth"},["q"]="How do people travel between Zephras Isle and Azeroth?"},{["a"]="The Skyborne are the inhabitants who make their home on Zephras Isle and its sister sky-realms. Having dwelled in Skywall for generations, they now face the slow destruction of their flying homeland as its binding magic fades.",["al"]={"skyborne race","who lives on zephras"},["q"]="Who are the Skyborne?"},{["a"]="Aside from peaceful villages and visiting refugees, the island is troubled by local bandits, cavern-dwelling nightclaws, and beasts inhabiting areas like the Shriekling Den and Nightclaw Cavern.",["al"]={"zephras inhabitants","enemies on zephras"},["q"]="Who lives on Zephras Isle besides the Skyborne?"}},["kw"]={"airships","anvilas","elemental plane","siroccas","skyborne","skywall","valanaar","windstones","zephras"},["n"]="Zephras Isle",["s"]="Zephras Isle is a flying island suspended within Skywall, the Elemental Plane of air. It serves as the ancestral starting home of the Skyborne, whose society now faces an existential crisis as the island's sustaining magic steadily erodes. With contact severed from neighboring sky-realms, its inhabitants struggle against elemental collapse and arriving refugees.",["sec"]={{["b"]="Zephras Isle is an isolated landmass adrift in the endless winds of Skywall. For generations, the Skyborne lived here alongside native air elementals who helped preserve the magical currents holding the isle aloft. However, several centuries ago, those elemental stewards vanished entirely. Deprived of their presence and guardianship, the latent enchantments upholding the land have slowly begun to fail, causing Zephras to slowly fracture and destabilize.",["t"]="A Realm Aloft"},{["b"]="Zephras was not the only sanctuary inhabited by the Skyborne within the Elemental Plane. Sister islands known as Anvilas and Siroccas also drifted across Skywall, but communications between them ceased after the elementals vanished. In recent times, refugees from these crumbling neighboring landmasses have managed to reach Zephras seeking safety, bearing grim tidings that the other islands are disintegrating outright beneath their feet.",["t"]="Severed Horizons"},{["b"]="With their own landmass in slow decay, the Skyborne of Zephras have begun looking beyond the boundaries of Skywall toward Azeroth. From the skydocks of Valanaar, airships now venture forth to establish rare lifelines to the mortal realm. These voyages connect the airy domain directly to points on Azeroth, including the outskirts of Dalaran in the Alterac Mountains and the Skywatcher Plateau in Mulgore.",["t"]="Threshold to Azeroth"}}}}}
